ARTICLES.en = [
 {"id":"phishing-email","t":"How to recognize a phishing email","r":"3 min read","tag":"Phishing","date":"August 2026","paras":["Phishing is a scam in which an attacker pretends to be someone you trust, such as a bank, a delivery company, your school or a colleague, in order to make you hand over information or take a harmful action. Email is the classic channel, and it remains the most common starting point for account theft and malware infections.","## What a phishing email wants from you","The message is built to produce one of a few actions: enter a password on a fake page, open an attachment that installs malware, pay an invoice, buy gift cards, or reply with personal details. Everything else in the email, the logo, the polite tone and the urgent deadline, exists to make that action feel normal.","## Warning signs","- Urgency or threats: your account will be closed in 24 hours, a payment has failed, you must act now.\n- A sender address that does not match the organisation, or that is slightly misspelled.\n- Generic greetings such as dear customer, in a message that claims to know your account.\n- Links whose real destination differs from the text. Hover over a link, or press and hold on a phone, to see where it goes.\n- Unexpected attachments, especially archives, invoices and documents that ask you to enable macros.\n- Requests for passwords, codes, card numbers or payments by unusual methods.\n- Offers that are too good to be true, or messages about something you never ordered.","## Do not trust the display name","The name shown next to an email is easy to fake. Always look at the actual address, and remember that attackers may also register domains that look similar to the real one by changing a letter or adding extra words.","## What to do with a suspicious email","- Do not click links or open attachments.\n- Do not reply, and never send a password or a verification code.\n- Check through a separate route: open the official website or app yourself, or call the organisation using a number you already know.\n- Report the message as phishing to your email provider, your school or your IT team, and then delete it.","## If you already clicked","Do not panic, but act quickly. If you entered a password, change it right away and on any other site where you used it. Turn on two-factor authentication, check your account for unusual activity and, if you opened an attachment, run a security scan. Tell your school or employer so that others can be warned.","## Key takeaway","Phishing works by creating pressure. Slow down, check the sender and the link, and confirm requests through a channel you chose yourself."],"quiz":{"q":"You get an email saying your account will be permanently deleted in 2 hours unless you verify now. What should you do first?","options":["Click the link immediately to avoid losing the account","Reply with your password to confirm you are the owner","Check the sender address and the real domain before doing anything","Forward it to five friends to ask their opinion"],"correct":2,"explain":"Urgency is a manipulation tactic. Before doing anything, check the real sender address and log in directly through the official site or app instead of the email link."},"cat":2},
 {"id":"password-strength","t":"How strong is your password, really?","r":"3 min read","tag":"Passwords","date":"August 2026","paras":["A password is only as strong as the effort it takes someone else to guess it. Attackers rarely sit and type guesses by hand. They use software that can test millions of combinations, starting with the passwords people use most often. Strength is therefore about unpredictability and length, not about looking complicated.","## What makes a password weak","- Common choices such as 123456, password and qwerty, which appear at the top of every list of leaked passwords.\n- Personal details: names, birth dates, phone numbers or favourite teams.\n- Predictable tricks such as replacing a with @ or adding 1 at the end. Cracking tools already try these patterns.\n- Short length, even when it mixes letters, numbers and symbols.","## Length beats cleverness","Each extra character multiplies the number of possible combinations. A long password made of random words is much harder to crack than a short one packed with symbols, and it is easier to remember. Many security guidelines today recommend long passphrases and discourage forced complexity rules and regular changes that push people towards predictable patterns.","## Unique for every account","Even a very strong password stops being safe if you use it in more than one place. When one website is breached, attackers try the leaked passwords on other services. Using a different password everywhere means that a single leak stays a single leak.","## How to check and improve","- Aim for at least 14 to 16 characters for important accounts such as email and banking.\n- Use a random passphrase of several unrelated words, or let a password manager generate and store a random one.\n- Check whether your email has appeared in known breaches using a reputable breach-checking service, without entering your actual password.\n- Change a password immediately if a service reports a breach or if you notice unusual activity.","## Do not rely on a password alone","Even the best password can be stolen by a fake website or leaked by a company. Add two-factor authentication on accounts that matter, so that a stolen password is not enough.","## Key takeaway","A strong password is long, unpredictable and used only once. Combine that habit with a password manager and two-factor authentication, and most password attacks will fail."],"quiz":{"q":"Which password is likely the strongest against automated cracking tools?","options":["P@ssw0rd!","correct-horse-battery-lamp","123456789","qwerty123"],"correct":1,"explain":"Length and unpredictability beat forced complexity. A long, random multi-word phrase is far harder to crack than a short password with substituted symbols."},"cat":1},
 {"id":"common-mistakes","t":"10 common cybersecurity mistakes students make","r":"7 min read","tag":"Basics","date":"August 2026","paras":["1. Reusing the same password everywhere — one leaked site compromises every account that shares it.","2. Skipping two-factor authentication because it feels like an extra step, even on email and banking apps.","3. Clicking links in messages before checking who actually sent them, especially on unfamiliar Wi-Fi.","4. Ignoring software updates, which often patch security holes attackers already know about.","5. Oversharing on social media — birthdates and pet names are often the same details used in security questions.","6. Connecting to public Wi-Fi for anything sensitive without a VPN.","7. Downloading free versions of paid apps from unofficial sites, a common way malware spreads.","8. Leaving devices unlocked in shared spaces like libraries or dormitories.","9. Trusting a message just because it appears to come from a friend, since many attacks spread by hijacking real accounts.","10. Assuming it will not happen to me. Attackers usually cast a wide net rather than choosing victims individually."],"quiz":{"q":"One of your accounts appears in a data breach. Why is reusing the same password everywhere so risky?","options":["Sites delete any account whose password is shared","Reused passwords make each login slower to complete","One leaked password can open every account that shares it","Password managers refuse to store duplicate passwords"],"correct":2,"explain":"Attackers automatically try leaked passwords on other sites, so a single breach can unlock many accounts. Use a unique password for each account."},"cat":0},
 {"id":"social-engineering","t":"What social engineering looks like in real life","r":"6 min read","tag":"Social engineering","date":"August 2026","paras":["Social engineering skips the technical hacking entirely and targets people directly. Instead of breaking a system, an attacker convinces someone with legitimate access to open the door for them.","A common example is a phone call pretending to be tech support, asking you to install \"remote assistance\" software to fix a fake problem. Once installed, the attacker has full access to your device.","Another version happens over social media: a message from what looks like a friend account, urgently asking for money because they are \"stuck abroad.\" The account was likely hijacked earlier through a separate phishing attempt.","These attacks work because they exploit normal, healthy instincts — helpfulness, respect for authority, and trust in people we know. The defense is not paranoia, it is verification: hang up and call the company back on their official number, or confirm with a friend through a different channel before sending money.","If something creates pressure to act fast and skip your normal checks, that pressure is itself a warning sign."],"quiz":{"q":"A caller says they are tech support and asks you to install a remote assistance tool. What is the best response?","options":["Hang up and call the company back on its official number","Install it, because support staff are trusted experts","Install it but only share your screen, never your files","Ask for their employee ID, then install the tool"],"correct":0,"explain":"Remote tools give full control of your device. Verification means ending the call and contacting the company yourself through an official channel."},"cat":5},
 {"id":"securing-social-media","t":"A quick checklist for securing your social media accounts","r":"5 min read","tag":"Online security","date":"August 2026","paras":["Social accounts are often the first target, because they hold both personal information and a trusted identity attackers can impersonate.","Start with two-factor authentication using an authenticator app rather than SMS where possible, since SMS codes can sometimes be intercepted through SIM-swap attacks.","Review connected apps and third-party logins periodically. Old apps you no longer use can still have access to your account and data.","Set a strong, unique password for each platform, and check whether your email has appeared in any known data breaches.","Finally, be deliberate about what you share publicly. Birthdates, school names, and location tags all give attackers material for both phishing and security-question guessing."],"quiz":{"q":"Which two-factor option is generally safest for a social media account?","options":["SMS codes sent to your phone number","A security question about your first school","A second password that looks like the first one","An authenticator app that creates codes on your device"],"correct":3,"explain":"SMS codes can be intercepted through SIM-swap attacks. An authenticator app keeps the codes on your own device."},"cat":7},
 {"id":"public-wifi","t":"Why you should think twice before using public Wi-Fi","r":"3 min read","tag":"Internet security","date":"August 2026","paras":["Free Wi-Fi in a cafe, airport, hotel or library is convenient, but it is a shared network that you do not control. Anyone else connected to it, and the person who runs it, may be able to see or interfere with some of your traffic. That does not mean you can never use it, but it changes what you should do on it.","## The main risks","- Eavesdropping: on an open network without a password, data that is not encrypted can be read by others on the same network.\n- Fake hotspots, sometimes called evil twins: an attacker creates a network with a name like Airport_Free_WiFi and waits for people to join. Everything you send passes through their device.\n- Man-in-the-middle attacks: the attacker sits between you and the website you are visiting, reading or altering what passes.\n- Malicious captive portals: a login page that asks for an email, a phone number or a password, or prompts you to install a program.\n- Other devices on the network may try to connect to yours if file sharing is turned on.","## What HTTPS does and does not do","Websites that use HTTPS encrypt the connection between your browser and the site, which protects the content even on an untrusted network. Your browser also warns you if a certificate looks wrong. This is why modern public Wi-Fi is less dangerous than it used to be. But not every app or connection uses encryption consistently, and encryption does not protect you from a fake website that you are persuaded to use.","## Safer habits","- Confirm the exact network name with staff before joining.\n- Avoid banking, shopping and sensitive accounts on public Wi-Fi. Use mobile data when you need to do something important.\n- Make sure that the address shows https and that your browser shows no certificate warning.\n- Turn off automatic connection to open networks, and turn off file sharing.\n- Use a reputable VPN to encrypt all of your traffic on the network.\n- Keep your device updated, and log out of accounts when you finish.\n- Never ignore a certificate warning, and never install anything requested by a Wi-Fi login page.","## If you think you were affected","Disconnect, change the passwords of any accounts you used on that network from a safe connection, check for unusual activity and enable two-factor authentication.","## Key takeaway","Treat public Wi-Fi as an untrusted network. Use encrypted sites, a VPN or mobile data for anything private, and avoid doing sensitive tasks on it when you can."],"quiz":{"q":"You must check your bank balance while sitting in a cafe. What is the safest choice?","options":["Join whichever \"Free WiFi\" network has the strongest signal","Use your mobile data instead of the cafe network","Turn off your screen lock so the phone connects faster","Log in quickly and hope nobody is watching"],"correct":1,"explain":"Fake hotspots and unsecured networks let others see your traffic. Mobile data avoids the shared network completely."},"cat":4},
 {"id":"2fa-explained","t":"Two-factor authentication, explained simply","r":"3 min read","tag":"Passwords","date":"August 2026","paras":["Two-factor authentication, often shortened to 2FA, protects an account with two different kinds of proof instead of one. Even if an attacker learns your password, they still cannot sign in without the second factor.","## The idea behind it","Authentication factors fall into three groups: something you know, such as a password; something you have, such as your phone or a security key; and something you are, such as a fingerprint. Two-factor authentication combines two of these. A stolen password is something an attacker can have from anywhere in the world, but they are unlikely to also have your phone in their hand.","## Types of second factor","- Authenticator apps generate a short code that changes every thirty seconds or so. The codes are created on your device and do not travel over the phone network.\n- Hardware security keys are small devices that you plug in or tap. They are the strongest option and resist fake websites because they check the real address.\n- Passkeys replace the password with a cryptographic key stored on your device and unlocked by a fingerprint, face or PIN.\n- Text message codes are better than nothing but weaker, because messages can be intercepted, for instance through SIM-swap attacks.\n- Approval prompts on your phone are convenient, but beware of prompt bombing, in which an attacker sends repeated requests hoping you tap approve.","## Where to turn it on first","Start with your email, because it can reset almost every other account. Then enable it for banking and payment services, cloud storage, social media, gaming and school or work accounts.","## Setting it up well","Save your backup or recovery codes in a safe place, such as a password manager or a printed copy kept somewhere secure. Without them, losing your phone can lock you out of your account. Review which devices are trusted and remove old ones.","## Never share a code","No genuine service will call or message you to ask for a verification code. If someone asks you to read one out, it is a scam, and the code is the last thing standing between them and your account.","## Key takeaway","Two-factor authentication makes stolen passwords far less useful. Turn it on for your email first, prefer an authenticator app or security key to text messages, and keep your recovery codes safe."],"quiz":{"q":"Why is it wise to turn on two-factor authentication on your email account first?","options":["Email is the only kind of account that supports 2FA","It stops other people from sending you spam messages","Email can reset the passwords of most of your other accounts","It gives your mailbox extra storage space for free"],"correct":2,"explain":"Email is the recovery key to almost everything else, so protecting it protects your other accounts too."},"cat":1},
 {"id":"ransomware-explained","t":"What is ransomware, and how do you avoid it","r":"3 min read","tag":"Malware","date":"August 2026","paras":["Ransomware is malware that locks or encrypts your files, or your whole device, and demands payment to restore access. It is a business for criminals: they make money from victims who feel they have no other choice. Increasingly, attackers also steal copies of the data first and threaten to publish it, a tactic known as double extortion.","## How ransomware arrives","- Phishing emails with malicious attachments or links.\n- Fake software downloads, cracked programs and infected adverts.\n- Infected USB drives.\n- Remote access services with weak or reused passwords.\n- Unpatched software that the attacker can exploit.","## What happens when it strikes","Files are encrypted and renamed, and a message appears with instructions to pay, usually in cryptocurrency, within a deadline. Without the key, the encrypted files are normally unreadable. Hospitals, schools, companies and individuals have all been affected, and some attacks have caused serious disruption.","## Should you pay?","Authorities and security experts advise against paying. There is no guarantee that you will get a working key, payment encourages more attacks, and you may be marked as someone willing to pay again. If you are a victim, report the incident to the police or the relevant national cyber agency, and look for free decryption tools from trusted sources, which exist for some older ransomware families.","## Prevention is the real defence","- Keep regular backups, with at least one copy disconnected from your computer or in a service that keeps previous versions. A backup that is always connected can be encrypted along with everything else.\n- Install updates for your system and programs.\n- Be careful with email attachments, links and downloads.\n- Use unique passwords and two-factor authentication on remote access and cloud accounts.\n- Use security software with ransomware protection.\n- Do not run programs with administrator rights for daily work.","## If your device is infected","Disconnect it from the network and Wi-Fi right away to limit spread, do not switch it off if you are told to preserve evidence, and seek help. After cleaning the device, restore the files from a backup, then change your passwords.","## Key takeaway","Good backups turn ransomware from a disaster into an inconvenience. Keep them separate, test that they work, and keep your system updated."],"quiz":{"q":"Which habit protects you best against ransomware before an attack happens?","options":["Regular offline backups and keeping software updated","Paying quickly so the attackers stay friendly","Renaming your files so malware cannot find them","Keeping every backup drive plugged in all the time"],"correct":0,"explain":"Backups kept away from your computer let you restore files without paying, and updates close the holes ransomware uses."},"cat":3},
 {"id":"fake-websites","t":"How to recognize fake websites and cloned login pages","r":"3 min read","tag":"Phishing","date":"August 2026","paras":["A fake website is built to look like a real one in order to steal your password, payment details or personal information. The most dangerous kind is the cloned login page, which can copy the logo, colours and layout of a real service exactly.","## Why they work","Visual design is easy to copy. Attackers can save a login page, change where the form data is sent and publish it on their own domain. If you arrive there by clicking a link in a message, a search advertisement or a mistyped address, the page can look completely authentic.","## The one thing that cannot be copied","The domain name in the address bar is controlled by whoever owns it, so a fake site must use a different one. This is the most reliable check you have. Read it carefully, from right to left up to the first single slash. The real company name has to appear immediately before the final part of the domain. A page at accounts.example.com belongs to example.com, but a page at example.com.login-check.net belongs to login-check.net.","## Common tricks","- Lookalike spellings, such as a letter replaced with a similar character or an extra letter.\n- Extra words around the brand, for instance a service name followed by security, verify or support.\n- Unusual endings such as .top, .xyz or .info on a page that claims to be a major company, although endings alone prove nothing.\n- A padlock icon. A padlock only means that the connection is encrypted, and criminals can get certificates for fake sites too.\n- Pop-ups or redirects that appear after you click an advert or a message.","## Safe habits","- Bookmark the login pages of your bank, email and school portal, and use the bookmarks instead of links.\n- Type addresses yourself for important services.\n- Use a password manager: it will not fill in a password on a domain it does not recognise.\n- Treat any page that asks for a verification code or card details after a message as suspicious.\n- Turn on two-factor authentication, ideally with a security key or passkey, which refuse to work on fake sites.","## If you entered details on a fake site","Change the password immediately, on the real site and anywhere you reused it. Contact your bank if you entered card details, and review account activity for anything unfamiliar.","## Key takeaway","Do not judge a page by how it looks. Check the domain, reach important sites through bookmarks, and let your password manager act as a second pair of eyes."],"quiz":{"q":"A login page looks identical to your bank's. What is the most reliable thing to check?","options":["The logo and colors used on the page","Whether the page loads quickly on your phone","How professional the text on the page looks","The domain name shown in the address bar"],"correct":3,"explain":"A cloned page can copy everything except the real domain name. Use bookmarks for important logins."},"cat":2},
 {"id":"how-vpn-works","t":"How VPNs actually work","r":"3 min read","tag":"Internet security","date":"August 2026","paras":["A VPN, or virtual private network, creates an encrypted tunnel between your device and a server run by the VPN provider. Your traffic travels through that tunnel before reaching the rest of the internet, which hides it from the network you are connected to.","## What a VPN does","- It encrypts the data between your device and the VPN server, so someone on the same Wi-Fi network cannot read it.\n- It hides your real IP address from the websites you visit, which see the VPN server's address instead.\n- It prevents your internet provider from seeing which websites you visit, although it still sees that you are connected to a VPN.\n- It can make it appear that you are in another country, which is useful for travel, although using it to bypass rules or licences may breach a service's terms.","## What a VPN does not do","A VPN is not a magic shield. It does not make you anonymous: the VPN provider can see your traffic, and websites can still identify you if you log in or use cookies. It does not protect you from malware you download or from phishing. It does not make a bad website safe, and it does not replace strong passwords and updates.","## Trust moves, it does not disappear","Using a VPN shifts trust from your internet provider to the VPN company. That is why the choice of provider matters. Be careful with free VPNs, because some earn money by showing adverts or selling data, and a few have been found to contain malware. Look for a provider with a clear privacy policy, a record of independent audits and a reputation that can be checked.","## When it is useful","- On public Wi-Fi, where it protects the traffic of apps that do not use encryption.\n- When you want to keep your browsing private from the network operator.\n- When working remotely and connecting to a school or company network, which often requires a VPN.\n- When travelling in places where some sites are restricted, subject to local law.","## How to choose","- Pick a well-reviewed paid service or one run by an organisation you trust.\n- Check the no-logs claims and whether they have been audited.\n- Use up-to-date apps with modern protocols, and enable the kill switch that blocks traffic if the VPN drops.","## Key takeaway","A VPN protects the path between you and the VPN server. It is a helpful tool on public networks, but it is not anonymity and it is not a replacement for careful behaviour."],"quiz":{"q":"Which statement about VPNs is true?","options":["It makes you completely anonymous everywhere online","It encrypts your traffic but cannot stop you being phished","It protects your device from every kind of malware","It removes the need to use strong passwords"],"correct":1,"explain":"A VPN hides your traffic from the local network, but you can still be tricked into handing over information."},"cat":4},
 {"id":"encryption-plain-language","t":"Understanding encryption in plain language","r":"3 min read","tag":"Basics","date":"August 2026","paras":["Encryption is the technique of scrambling information so that only someone with the right key can read it. Without the key, encrypted data looks like random noise. It is one of the most important tools in digital security, and you already use it many times a day without noticing.","## Where you already use it","Whenever a website address begins with https, the connection between your browser and the site is encrypted. Messaging apps with end-to-end encryption scramble messages so that only you and the person you are talking to can read them. Your phone encrypts its storage when you set a screen lock, and Wi-Fi networks protected by a password use encryption between your device and the router.","## Symmetric encryption","In symmetric encryption, the same secret key locks and unlocks the data. It is fast and is used to protect large amounts of information, such as the contents of a disk or the data flowing through a secure connection. Its weakness is sharing: both sides need the same key, and sending it safely is a problem in itself.","## Asymmetric encryption","Asymmetric encryption uses a pair of keys. A public key can be shared with anyone and is used to lock data or check a signature. A private key stays secret and is the only thing that can unlock data locked with the matching public key. This solves the key-sharing problem, and it is how your browser and a website agree on a secret to use for the rest of the session. In practice, secure connections combine both types: asymmetric encryption to agree on a key, then symmetric encryption for speed.","## What encryption does not do","Encryption protects information while it is stored or travelling, but it does not make you invisible and it does not stop you from being tricked. If you give your password to a fake website, encryption cannot help, and a message you forward to the wrong person is still readable by them. Lost or weak keys are also a danger: encrypted data without its key is usually gone for good.","## Practical habits","- Look for https before entering passwords or card details.\n- Use a screen lock so your phone storage is encrypted and protected.\n- Choose messaging apps with end-to-end encryption for private conversations.\n- Keep a secure backup of recovery keys for anything encrypted.","## Key takeaway","Encryption is a lock that works on data. Used correctly, it keeps private information private even if someone intercepts it, but it is only one part of staying safe."],"quiz":{"q":"In asymmetric encryption, how is data locked and unlocked?","options":["A public key locks it and a matching private key unlocks it","The same single key both locks and unlocks the data","A password locks it and a fingerprint unlocks it","No key is needed once the data has been scrambled"],"correct":0,"explain":"Asymmetric encryption uses a key pair. This is how HTTPS keeps your browsing private."},"cat":0},
 {"id":"sim-swap","t":"SIM-swap attacks and how to prevent them","r":"5 min read","tag":"Social engineering","date":"August 2026","paras":["A SIM-swap attack happens when someone tricks your mobile carrier into transferring your phone number to their SIM card, often using personal details gathered from social media or previous leaks.","Once they control your number, they can intercept SMS-based verification codes and reset passwords on accounts tied to that phone number, including banking apps.","Protect yourself by adding a PIN or extra security question with your mobile carrier, and prefer authenticator apps over SMS for two-factor authentication wherever possible."],"quiz":{"q":"What can an attacker do after a successful SIM swap on your number?","options":["Read all of the photos stored on your phone","Make your phone battery drain much faster","Receive your SMS codes and reset your account passwords","See every website you visited during last year"],"correct":2,"explain":"Once they control your number, SMS verification codes reach them. Add a PIN with your carrier and prefer authenticator apps."},"cat":5},
 {"id":"safe-online-shopping","t":"Safe online shopping habits","r":"5 min read","tag":"Online security","date":"August 2026","paras":["Before entering payment details on an unfamiliar store, check for HTTPS, a real physical address or contact page, and reviews from sources outside the site itself.","Use a credit card or a virtual card number rather than a debit card where possible, since credit cards generally offer stronger fraud protection and dispute options.","Be wary of deals that seem far too good to be true, and double-check the domain name carefully — many scam stores use names that closely mimic well-known brands."],"quiz":{"q":"Which payment method usually gives stronger fraud protection online?","options":["A debit card linked to your main bank account","A bank transfer sent directly to the seller","Gift cards bought through the seller's own page","A credit card or a virtual card number"],"correct":3,"explain":"Credit cards offer stronger dispute options, and virtual numbers keep your real card details away from the shop."},"cat":4},
 {"id":"gaming-account-security","t":"Protecting your identity on gaming platforms","r":"5 min read","tag":"Online security","date":"August 2026","paras":["Gaming accounts are frequently targeted because they hold purchased items, in-game currency, and linked payment methods that can be resold.","Enable 2FA on your gaming platform and email, avoid downloading \"cheats\" or \"mods\" from unofficial sources, since these are a common way malware spreads among gamers.","Be cautious of messages from other players offering free items in exchange for your login details or a \"trade\" link — these almost always lead to phishing pages designed to look like the real platform."],"quiz":{"q":"Another player offers free items if you click their \"trade\" link. What is most likely?","options":["A reward from the game's developers for loyal players","A phishing page copying the platform to steal your login","A harmless link that only opens the trade window","A test the platform uses to check your account"],"correct":1,"explain":"Free-item offers almost always lead to pages that imitate the real platform and steal your login details."},"cat":1},
 {"id":"account-hacked","t":"What to do if your account gets hacked","r":"6 min read","tag":"Basics","date":"August 2026","paras":["Act quickly: change your password immediately from a different, trusted device, and log out of all active sessions if the platform allows it.","Enable two-factor authentication if you have not already, and check your account’s recovery email and phone number, since attackers often change these first to lock you out permanently.","Check for suspicious activity — messages sent, purchases made, or new connected apps — and report the compromise to the platform. If you reused that password anywhere else, change it there too immediately."],"quiz":{"q":"Your account has been hacked. What should you do first?","options":["Change the password from a different, trusted device","Wait a few days to see what the attacker does","Delete the account without contacting the platform","Message the attacker and ask them to stop"],"correct":0,"explain":"Act quickly from a clean device, log out all sessions, and check your recovery email and phone number."},"cat":1},
 {"id":"data-breaches","t":"Understanding data breaches and what to do after one","r":"6 min read","tag":"Basics","date":"August 2026","paras":["A data breach happens when a company’s systems are compromised and user data — emails, passwords, sometimes payment details — is stolen, often later sold or leaked online.","You can check whether your email has appeared in known breaches using reputable breach-checking services, without ever needing to enter your actual password.","If you are affected, change the password on that account and anywhere you reused it, and watch closely for follow-up phishing attempts, since breached data is often used to craft convincing scam emails."],"quiz":{"q":"You find your email in a data breach. What is the right next step?","options":["Ignore it, because old breaches are harmless","Change only the name of your email and keep the password","Change reused passwords and watch for phishing","Send your password to the checking site to confirm it"],"correct":2,"explain":"Breached data is used to craft convincing scam emails. Genuine breach checkers never need your actual password."},"cat":7},
 {"id":"mobile-security-basics","t":"Mobile security basics for smartphones","r":"5 min read","tag":"Internet security","date":"August 2026","paras":["Your phone holds more personal data than most computers, yet often gets fewer security precautions. Start with a strong screen lock — a PIN or biometric, not a simple pattern.","Only install apps from official app stores, and review the permissions an app requests; a flashlight app rarely needs access to your contacts or messages.","Keep your operating system updated, since updates frequently patch security vulnerabilities that are actively being exploited."],"quiz":{"q":"A flashlight app asks for access to your contacts and messages. What should you do?","options":["Allow it, since every app needs full access","Deny it, because a flashlight does not need that data","Allow it only if the app is free to download","Allow it and uninstall the app a few weeks later"],"correct":1,"explain":"Apps should only get the permissions their job requires. Excess permissions are a warning sign."},"cat":6},
 {"id":"usb-safety","t":"Safe use of USB drives and external devices","r":"4 min read","tag":"Malware","date":"August 2026","paras":["An unfamiliar USB drive found in a parking lot or classroom is a classic attack tool: plugging it in can silently install malware or trigger scripted keystrokes.","Never plug in a USB drive of unknown origin, even out of curiosity, and be cautious with borrowed drives, scanning them with antivirus before opening files.","For your own drives, consider encrypting sensitive files, since a lost USB drive can expose everything on it to whoever finds it."],"quiz":{"q":"You find a USB drive in a parking lot. What is the safest action?","options":["Plug it in on an old computer you no longer use","Plug it in to look for the owner's name","Plug it in after switching off the Wi-Fi","Do not plug it in, however curious you are"],"correct":3,"explain":"An unknown USB drive can silently install malware or run scripted keystrokes as soon as it is connected."},"cat":3},
 {"id":"fake-job-offers","t":"How to spot a fake job offer scam","r":"5 min read","tag":"Phishing","date":"August 2026","paras":["Fake job offers often arrive unexpectedly, promise unusually high pay for simple work, and move very quickly toward an \"interview\" over chat rather than a real hiring process.","A major warning sign is being asked to pay for training materials, equipment, or a \"processing fee\" before starting — legitimate employers do not charge candidates to be hired.","Verify the company independently: search for it outside of the messages they sent you, check whether the recruiter’s profile and domain match a real organization, and never share your ID, bank details, or passport scan before verifying legitimacy."],"quiz":{"q":"A \"recruiter\" offers you a job but asks for a $50 processing fee first. What does this suggest?","options":["A scam, since real employers do not charge candidates","A normal step that shows you are serious","A security deposit that every employer asks for","A sign that the job pays unusually well"],"correct":0,"explain":"Legitimate employers never charge you to be hired. Verify the company independently before sharing any details."},"cat":2},
 {"id":"cyberbullying-safety","t":"Cyberbullying and online harassment: staying safe","r":"5 min read","tag":"Social engineering","date":"August 2026","paras":["Online harassment can include repeated unwanted messages, impersonation, doxxing (sharing private information publicly), or coordinated pile-ons across platforms.","Keep evidence: screenshot messages with timestamps before blocking or reporting, since platforms and, if needed, authorities will want that record.","Tighten your privacy settings, limit who can message or tag you, and talk to a trusted adult, teacher, or platform support — you do not have to handle harassment alone or in silence."],"quiz":{"q":"You are being harassed online. What should you do before blocking the person?","options":["Reply angrily so they stop immediately","Delete all the messages so you forget them","Take screenshots with dates and times as evidence","Post their personal details to embarrass them"],"correct":2,"explain":"Platforms and authorities will want a record, so save evidence first, then block, report and talk to someone you trust."},"cat":7},
 {"id":"backing-up-data","t":"Backing up your data the right way","r":"4 min read","tag":"Basics","date":"August 2026","paras":["A good backup strategy follows the 3-2-1 rule: keep at least three copies of your data, on two different types of storage, with one copy stored somewhere physically separate.","Cloud storage handles the offsite copy well, while an external drive covers the second local copy. Automate backups where possible, since a backup you forget to run is not a backup at all.","Test your backups occasionally by actually restoring a file — a backup that cannot be restored is just wasted storage space."],"quiz":{"q":"What does the 3-2-1 backup rule recommend?","options":["Three backups made on the same external drive","Three copies, on two storage types, with one stored offsite","Two copies in the cloud and one printed on paper","One copy, tested every two weeks, kept in three folders"],"correct":1,"explain":"Spreading copies across different storage types and locations means one failure cannot destroy everything."},"cat":0},
 {"id":"what-is-cybersecurity","t":"What is cybersecurity, and why does it matter to you?","r":"3 min read","tag":"Cybersecurity basics","date":"October 2026","paras":["Cybersecurity is the practice of protecting devices, networks, accounts and data from being accessed, changed or destroyed by people who are not supposed to touch them. It sounds like something for big companies and governments, but the part that affects most people is much smaller and closer to home: your phone, your email, your social media and your school or work accounts.","## What are we actually protecting?","Almost everything important in your digital life sits behind an account. Your email can reset the password of nearly every other service you use. Your phone holds photos, messages and banking apps. Your school or work account holds files that other people rely on. If an attacker gets into any of these, the damage is rarely limited to one device: it spreads to friends, classmates and colleagues who trust messages coming from you.","## Who attacks, and why?","Most attacks are not personal. Criminals send the same fake message to thousands of people and see who responds, because stolen accounts can be resold, used to send more scams, or used to steal money. A smaller number of attacks are targeted, for example against a person with access to money or sensitive information. Others come from curiosity, revenge or a wish to cause disruption. In all of these cases the attacker usually takes the easiest path, and the easiest path is more often a person than a machine.","## The three layers of defence","- People: knowing how scams work, and pausing before clicking, paying or sharing a code.\n- Technology: updates, strong and unique passwords, two-factor authentication, backups and security software.\n- Process: habits such as checking who is really asking, keeping recovery details up to date and knowing what to do when something goes wrong.","No single layer is enough. A perfect password does not help if you read the code out to a caller, and a careful person is still exposed if their phone is years out of date.","## Where to start","You do not need to become an expert. A small set of habits blocks most everyday attacks: use a different password for every account, turn on two-factor authentication starting with your email, install updates, back up what you cannot afford to lose, and treat unexpected messages that create urgency with suspicion. The rest of the topics on this site build on these ideas.","## Key takeaway","Cybersecurity is not about being unhackable. It is about making yourself a harder target than the next person and knowing how to recover quickly if something does go wrong."],"quiz":null,"cat":0},
 {"id":"cia-triad","t":"The CIA triad: confidentiality, integrity and availability","r":"3 min read","tag":"Cybersecurity basics","date":"October 2026","paras":["Security professionals often describe their goals with three words that form the CIA triad: confidentiality, integrity and availability. It has nothing to do with any intelligence agency. It is simply a checklist that helps you ask, for any system or piece of data, what could go wrong and what exactly you are trying to protect.","## Confidentiality: only the right people can see it","Confidentiality means information is visible only to those who are allowed to see it. Your messages, medical records, grades and bank details are all examples. The usual tools are passwords, encryption and access rules. A confidentiality failure is a data leak: someone reads or copies something they should not have.","## Integrity: it has not been changed without permission","Integrity means data stays accurate and unaltered unless an authorised person changes it. If an attacker could edit a bank balance, a grade, a software download or a medical dosage, nobody could trust the system even if nothing was leaked. Tools that protect integrity include permissions, digital signatures and checksums, which let you confirm that a downloaded file is exactly the file the publisher released.","## Availability: it is there when you need it","Availability means systems and data can be reached when needed. A website knocked offline by a flood of traffic, a laptop whose files are locked by ransomware, or a phone with no backup after it is lost are all availability failures. Defences include backups, spare capacity and updates that keep systems running reliably.","## Why three goals instead of one","The three goals can pull against each other. Locking a file away completely makes it confidential but not available. Making it easy for everyone to reach improves availability but harms confidentiality. Good security is a balance, and the right balance depends on what is being protected.","## Examples in everyday life","- A stranger reads your private chat: a confidentiality problem.\n- Someone edits a shared document and changes the numbers without telling anyone: an integrity problem.\n- Your school portal is down on the day of an exam: an availability problem.\n- Ransomware locks your files and demands payment: mainly availability, and often confidentiality if the data is also copied.","## Key takeaway","When you are unsure how to think about a risk, ask three questions: who should be able to see this, who should be able to change it, and what happens if I cannot get to it. Your answers show where protection matters most."],"quiz":null,"cat":0},
 {"id":"authentication","t":"Authentication: how systems check who you are","r":"3 min read","tag":"Cybersecurity basics","date":"October 2026","paras":["Authentication is the process of proving that you are who you claim to be. When you type a password, scan a fingerprint or approve a prompt on your phone, you are being authenticated. It is the first gate between an attacker and your account, which is why so many attacks try to get around it.","## The three kinds of proof","Authentication factors are usually grouped into three categories:\n- Something you know: a password, a PIN or the answer to a question.\n- Something you have: your phone, a hardware security key or a code generator.\n- Something you are: a fingerprint, face or other biometric.","A single factor, most often the password, is the weakest setup because anything you know can be guessed, stolen or tricked out of you. Combining two different kinds of factor is called multi-factor authentication and is far stronger, because the attacker needs both.","## Authentication is not the same as authorization","Authentication answers the question who are you. Authorization answers what are you allowed to do. They work together, but they are separate steps, and mixing them up leads to mistakes.","## How authentication gets attacked","Attackers rarely break the maths. They guess common passwords, reuse passwords leaked from other sites, send fake login pages, or trick people into handing over one-time codes. Some attacks also target the recovery process, for example resetting a password by taking over the email or phone number linked to the account.","## Making authentication stronger","- Use a long, unique password for every account and store it in a password manager.\n- Turn on two-factor authentication, preferring an authenticator app or security key to text messages.\n- Keep your recovery email and phone number up to date and well protected.\n- Never share a one-time code: legitimate services do not ask for it.","## Key takeaway","Strong authentication means more than a good password. It means combining different kinds of proof and protecting the recovery routes an attacker might use instead."],"quiz":null,"cat":0},
 {"id":"authorization","t":"Authorization: what you are allowed to do once you are in","r":"3 min read","tag":"Cybersecurity basics","date":"October 2026","paras":["After a system knows who you are, it still has to decide what you may do. That decision is authorization. Logging in to your school portal proves you are a student. Authorization is why you can see your own grades but not those of your classmates, and why a teacher can edit marks that you cannot.","## Permissions, roles and access control","Systems usually express authorization through permissions such as read, write, delete and share. To avoid managing permissions for every person one at a time, they are often grouped into roles, for example student, teacher or administrator. When someone joins, they are assigned a role, and their access follows from it.","## The principle of least privilege","The most important rule is least privilege: give each person or program only the access needed for its job and nothing more. If an account with limited access is stolen, the damage is limited too. If an administrator account is stolen, everything is at risk. This is why it is wise to use an everyday account for daily work rather than an all-powerful one.","## Where it appears in your own life","- App permissions: a flashlight app that asks for your contacts is requesting more than it needs.\n- Shared documents: viewing, commenting and editing are different levels of access.\n- Family computers: separate user accounts keep one person's mistakes from affecting everyone.\n- Social media: you decide which friends or apps can see your posts and information.","## Common failures","Authorization problems often come from permissions that are too broad, old accounts that were never removed, or files shared with anyone who has the link. Attackers also try privilege escalation, where a limited account is used to gain higher rights.","## How to apply it","Review who has access to your shared folders and connected apps from time to time. Remove access you no longer need, and when you share something, choose the smallest audience that works.","## Key takeaway","Authentication gets you through the door, but authorization decides which rooms you can enter. Keeping access narrow limits how much harm any single mistake or stolen password can cause."],"quiz":null,"cat":0},
 {"id":"digital-identity","t":"Digital identity: who you are online","r":"3 min read","tag":"Cybersecurity basics","date":"October 2026","paras":["Your digital identity is the collection of information that represents you online: your name, usernames, email addresses, phone number, photos, posts, purchase history and the accounts that prove you are you. Unlike a passport, it is spread across dozens of services, and no single organisation controls all of it.","## What makes up a digital identity","Some parts you create deliberately, such as profiles and posts. Others are collected without much thought on your part: location history, browsing habits, device details and the connections between your accounts. Identity also includes the credentials that unlock things, such as passwords, codes and the email address used for recovery.","## Why identity is valuable to attackers","Identity is what lets someone act as you. With enough details an attacker can reset passwords, open accounts, apply for credit, trick your friends or pass identity checks. Even small pieces matter: a birth date, a school name and a pet's name together can answer security questions or make a scam message sound convincing.","## The danger of one identity everywhere","Many people use one email address and one password for everything. If that single identity is compromised, every connected account falls with it. Using a single account to sign in to many services is convenient, but it also concentrates risk, so the account that acts as your key deserves the strongest protection available.","## Looking after your identity","- Protect your primary email with a unique password and two-factor authentication, because it is the recovery route for almost everything.\n- Use different usernames and email aliases for services you do not fully trust.\n- Share personal details sparingly, especially birth date, address and ID numbers.\n- Search for your own name from time to time to see what is public.\n- If a service you use is breached, change the password and watch for suspicious messages.","## Separate your public and private selves","It can help to keep a public identity for open profiles and a more private one for banking, school and family. This limits how easily your accounts can be connected to each other.","## Key takeaway","Your digital identity is a set of keys and clues about you. The fewer clues you give away and the better you protect the keys, the harder it is for someone else to pretend to be you."],"quiz":null,"cat":0},
 {"id":"risk-threat-vulnerability","t":"Risk, threat and vulnerability: three words people mix up","r":"3 min read","tag":"Cybersecurity basics","date":"October 2026","paras":["Security discussions use three terms that sound similar but mean different things: threat, vulnerability and risk. Understanding the difference makes it easier to decide what to worry about and what to fix first.","## Threat: what could cause harm","A threat is anything that could cause damage: a scammer, a piece of malware, a careless mistake, a power failure or a storm. A threat on its own does not guarantee harm. It only describes what might happen.","## Vulnerability: a weakness","A vulnerability is a weakness that a threat could use. Examples include software that has not been updated, a reused password, an unlocked phone or a habit of clicking links without checking them. Vulnerabilities can be technical, human or physical.","## Risk: the chance and the cost","Risk combines the two. It is the likelihood that a threat will take advantage of a vulnerability, multiplied by how bad the result would be. A scammer is a threat. Using the same password everywhere is a vulnerability. The risk is that one leaked password opens your email, bank and social accounts at the same time.","## A simple example","Imagine a laptop used for school work. The threat is a thief or a piece of malware. The vulnerabilities are an unlocked screen and no backup. The risk is losing assignments and exposing personal files. You can reduce risk by removing the vulnerability, with a screen lock and a backup, even though you cannot remove the threat.","## Managing risk","You cannot eliminate risk, but you can handle it in four ways:\n- Reduce it, by fixing the vulnerability.\n- Avoid it, by not doing the risky activity.\n- Transfer it, for example with insurance or by using a trusted service.\n- Accept it, when the cost of protection is greater than the possible harm.","## Prioritising","Because time and attention are limited, it makes sense to fix the biggest risks first: weaknesses that are easy to exploit and would cause serious damage. For most people that means passwords, updates, two-factor authentication and backups.","## Key takeaway","Threats are always going to exist, so security work focuses on vulnerabilities. Reduce the weaknesses that a likely threat could use, and you reduce the risk."],"quiz":null,"cat":0},
 {"id":"zero-trust","t":"Zero trust: never trust, always verify","r":"3 min read","tag":"Cybersecurity basics","date":"October 2026","paras":["Zero trust is a security approach built on one idea: no person, device or network should be trusted automatically, even if it is already inside your system. Instead, every request is checked each time. The common summary is never trust, always verify.","## The old model: a castle and a moat","Traditional networks worked like a castle with a moat. Everything outside was treated as dangerous, and anyone who got inside, through the office network or a company VPN, was largely trusted. The weakness is obvious. Once an attacker gets inside, for example with a stolen password, they can move around freely and reach data they were never meant to touch.","## The zero trust approach","Zero trust assumes that a breach could already be happening. It rests on a few principles:\n- Verify explicitly: check identity, device health and context every time, not only at login.\n- Use least privilege: give access only to what is needed, and only for as long as it is needed.\n- Assume breach: design systems so that a compromised account or device cannot reach everything.","## What it looks like in practice","Organisations that adopt zero trust typically use strong multi-factor authentication, divide their networks into small sections so that a problem in one does not spread, check that devices are updated before letting them connect, and log activity so that unusual behaviour is noticed.","## Zero trust in your own life","You can apply the same thinking at home. Do not trust a message just because it appears to come from a friend or a bank, because accounts get hijacked and sender names are easy to fake. Do not trust a Wi-Fi network just because it has a familiar name. Check through a separate route: call the person, open the official app yourself, or type the website address directly.","## Limits","Zero trust is not a product you buy and switch on. It is a way of designing systems and habits, and it takes planning. For individuals, the cost is a few extra seconds of checking, which is small compared with the cost of a successful scam.","## Key takeaway","Trust should be earned each time and given in small amounts. Verifying before you act keeps one mistake or one stolen password from becoming a full compromise."],"quiz":null,"cat":0},
 {"id":"password-managers","t":"Password managers: one strong master password instead of dozens","r":"3 min read","tag":"Password security","date":"October 2026","paras":["Most people have far more accounts than they can remember passwords for. The usual result is short, similar or reused passwords, which is exactly what attackers hope for. A password manager solves this by remembering everything for you, so you only have to memorise one strong password.","## What a password manager does","A password manager is an app or browser feature that stores your logins in an encrypted vault. It can generate long random passwords, fill them in automatically and warn you about weak, reused or leaked ones. Because it fills in credentials only on the correct website address, it can also help you notice a fake login page: if the manager does not offer your password, check the address carefully.","## How it keeps data safe","The vault is encrypted with a key derived from your master password. Well-designed services cannot read your vault themselves, which means a breach at the company should not expose your passwords in plain form. Your master password is therefore the key to everything, so it must be long, unique and never reused anywhere else.","## Common worries","- Is it safe to put everything in one place? It is generally safer than reusing weak passwords, provided the master password is strong and the account has two-factor authentication.\n- What if I forget the master password? Most managers cannot recover it for you, so store a recovery key or backup somewhere safe.\n- What if the service is hacked? Encrypted vaults are designed to stay unreadable, but this is another reason to choose a reputable, well-reviewed provider.","## Getting started","- Pick a reputable manager, including the one built into a trusted browser or operating system if you prefer.\n- Create a long master password or passphrase and turn on two-factor authentication.\n- Start with your email, banking and main social accounts, replacing old passwords with generated ones.\n- Use the security report to find reused or leaked passwords and change those first.","## Key takeaway","A password manager lets you use a different, long, random password on every account without having to remember any of them. It is one of the most effective security upgrades available to ordinary users."],"quiz":null,"cat":1},
 {"id":"password-reuse","t":"Password reuse: why one leak can open many doors","r":"3 min read","tag":"Password security","date":"October 2026","paras":["Reusing a password feels harmless because it is easier than remembering many. The danger is that the strength of your password no longer matters: your accounts are only as safe as the least secure website where you used it.","## How a single leak becomes many","Websites are breached regularly, and stolen login details are often sold or shared online. Attackers then try the same email and password on other popular services, such as email, shopping, banking and social media. This is called credential stuffing, and it is automated, so thousands of accounts can be tested in a short time. If you used the same password, the attacker simply walks in.","## Small sites, big consequences","People often reuse a strong password on important accounts and a weak one on forums or shops. The attacker does not need to break your bank. They only need the forum, which is likely to be much less protected, and then rely on you having used the same details elsewhere.","## Variations are not enough","Changing one character or adding the name of the website to a base password is only slightly better than reuse. Attackers know these patterns and tools test them automatically.","## What to do instead","- Use a different password for every account, and let a password manager create and remember them.\n- Start by fixing the accounts that matter most: email, banking, school or work, and social media.\n- Turn on two-factor authentication so that a reused password alone is not enough to get in.\n- Check whether your email appears in known breaches and change any password that was exposed.","## Why email comes first","Your email address is the recovery route for most other accounts. If an attacker controls it, they can reset your other passwords one by one. Make sure your email password is unique and protected with a second factor.","## Key takeaway","Every reused password links your accounts together. Give each account its own password and a leak at one website stays contained."],"quiz":null,"cat":1},
 {"id":"brute-force-attacks","t":"Brute-force attacks: how passwords get guessed","r":"3 min read","tag":"Password security","date":"October 2026","paras":["A brute-force attack tries many possible passwords until one works. It is not clever, but it is effective against weak passwords because computers can try guesses at enormous speed.","## How a brute-force attack works","In its simplest form the attacker tries every possible combination of characters. In practice they usually start with smarter guesses: a dictionary of common passwords, names, dates and common patterns such as a word followed by a number. This is called a dictionary attack. Combinations of both are common.","## Online and offline attacks","In an online attack the attacker guesses directly at a login page. Services slow this down by limiting attempts, adding delays or locking the account. In an offline attack the attacker has already stolen a database of password hashes from a breached service and can try guesses on their own hardware, with no limit on speed. That is why a weak password is dangerous even on a site that locks accounts.","## What decides how long it takes","The main factors are length and unpredictability. Every additional character multiplies the number of possibilities. A random password of many characters, or several unrelated words together, can take an impractically long time to guess, while a short or common password can fall almost instantly.","## Defences","- Use long, unique, unpredictable passwords.\n- Turn on two-factor authentication so that a correct guess alone is not enough.\n- Avoid passwords based on personal information.\n- Services should store passwords as salted hashes, limit login attempts and notify users of suspicious activity.","## Warning signs","Repeated login alerts, notifications that someone tried to sign in, or lockouts that you did not cause can indicate that someone is guessing your password. Treat them seriously: change the password and enable a second factor.","## Key takeaway","Brute-force attacks succeed against passwords that are short, common or predictable. Length, randomness and two-factor authentication make them impractical."],"quiz":null,"cat":1},
 {"id":"credential-stuffing","t":"Credential stuffing: when leaked passwords are used elsewhere","r":"3 min read","tag":"Password security","date":"October 2026","paras":["Credential stuffing is an attack in which criminals take lists of email addresses and passwords that leaked from one website and try them automatically on many others. It does not require breaking into any system: it simply relies on people reusing the same login details.","## How it works","Large collections of leaked credentials circulate online. An attacker feeds them into software that attempts to log in to popular services such as streaming sites, online shops, gaming platforms and email. Each successful login is an account takeover. The attacker may then drain stored balances, buy goods, steal personal information, send scams to the account's contacts or sell access to someone else.","## Why it is so common","It is cheap, automated and works because password reuse is widespread. The attacks can be spread across many addresses to look like ordinary visitors, so they are hard for services to spot immediately.","## Signs that you might be affected","- Login notifications or password reset emails you did not request.\n- Orders, subscriptions or gift card balances that you do not recognise.\n- Friends receiving odd messages from your account.\n- Your email address appearing in a breach checking service.","## How to protect yourself","- Use a unique password for every account, ideally generated by a password manager.\n- Turn on two-factor authentication, especially for email, shopping, payment and gaming accounts.\n- Change passwords after a breach and on any other service where you used the same one.\n- Remove accounts you no longer use, so there is less to attack.","## What services can do","Companies defend against credential stuffing with rate limiting, bot detection, checks against known leaked passwords and alerts for logins from unusual places. Users can help by treating those alerts seriously.","## Key takeaway","Credential stuffing works only because of reused passwords. A unique password per account and two-factor authentication turn a leaked list into a dead end."],"quiz":null,"cat":1},
 {"id":"passphrases","t":"Passphrases: long passwords you can actually remember","r":"3 min read","tag":"Password security","date":"October 2026","paras":["A passphrase is a password made of several words, such as a short random sentence. It tends to be both much longer and much easier to remember than a jumble of letters and symbols, which is why many modern security guidelines recommend them.","## Why length matters more than complexity","The number of possible passwords grows rapidly with every extra character. A phrase of four or five unrelated words contains many characters, so a program that guesses systematically has an enormous number of possibilities to work through. A short password with a symbol and a capital letter has far fewer.","## How to make a good passphrase","- Choose several words at random, rather than a famous quote, lyric or phrase with personal meaning. A list of words picked by dice or by a generator is better than words you invent, because humans choose predictably.\n- Use at least four words, and more for important accounts.\n- Do not use names, birthdays or anything someone could learn about you.\n- Optionally add a separator or a number in an unexpected place, but do not rely on this for strength.","## Examples of the idea","Words such as cactus, violin, harbour, ticket and orange combined together make a long, unusual phrase that is easy to picture and hard to guess. It would be unwise to use that exact example, because anything published widely is no longer random.","## Where passphrases are best","They work well for the passwords you must remember: your password manager master password, your device login and your main email. For everything else, let a password manager generate and store random passwords for you.","## Common mistakes","- Using a song lyric, a proverb or a line from a book.\n- Repeating the same passphrase across accounts.\n- Choosing words connected to your life, such as pet names and cities.\n- Writing it somewhere easy to find.","## Key takeaway","A passphrase made of several random words is easy to remember and difficult to crack. Use one for the few passwords that you have to memorise, and keep it unique."],"quiz":null,"cat":1},
 {"id":"security-questions","t":"Security questions: why they are a weak lock","r":"3 min read","tag":"Password security","date":"October 2026","paras":["Security questions such as your mother's maiden name, your first pet or the town where you were born were designed as a backup way to prove your identity when you forget a password. Today they are widely regarded as a weak point, and many security experts advise against relying on them.","## The problem: answers are easy to find or guess","Many answers are facts that other people know or can look up. Family names, schools, birthplaces and pet names often appear on social media, in public records or in old posts. Someone who knows you can answer them, and a determined attacker can research them. Other answers have a small number of likely possibilities: favourite colours, common first names and popular cities.","## Answers are static","Unlike a password, you cannot change your mother's maiden name. If an answer is exposed in one breach or quiz, it stays exposed everywhere you used it. The same answer often appears on several services, which links your accounts together.","## A better approach","- Treat the answer as another password. Give a random, unrelated answer, and store it in your password manager.\n- Do not use true answers. Someone who learns facts about you should not be able to use them.\n- Use different answers for different accounts.\n- If a service lets you skip security questions in favour of two-factor authentication or recovery codes, choose that option.","## Watch out for online quizzes","Social media quizzes that ask for your first car, your street or your first concert can gather the very answers used for account recovery. Avoid sharing these details in public posts and games.","## Recovery is a target","Attackers know that recovery routes are often easier to break than passwords. Keep your recovery email and phone number up to date and protected, so that you are the only person who can start a reset.","## Key takeaway","Security questions should never contain true, guessable facts. If you must use them, answer with random text kept in a password manager, and prefer stronger recovery methods whenever they are offered."],"quiz":null,"cat":1},
 {"id":"smishing","t":"Smishing: phishing by text message","r":"3 min read","tag":"Phishing","date":"October 2026","paras":["Smishing is phishing delivered by SMS or messaging apps. The name combines SMS and phishing. Text messages feel personal and urgent, people tend to open them quickly, and a small screen makes it harder to inspect links, so scammers use them heavily.","## Common smishing messages","- Delivery notices: a parcel could not be delivered, pay a small fee or confirm your address.\n- Bank alerts: suspicious activity on your card, call this number or log in using the link.\n- Prize or refund messages: you have won something or are owed money.\n- Account warnings: your phone, streaming or social account will be suspended.\n- Messages that start with wrong number or hello, building a conversation before a scam begins.","## Why the links are dangerous","The link usually leads to a convincing copy of a real website that asks for passwords, card details or one-time codes. In some cases it prompts you to install an app or a profile that gives the attacker access to your device. Shortened links hide the real destination, which makes them popular in these messages.","## How to recognise a smish","- It arrives from an unknown number or an unusual email-style sender, although numbers can also be faked.\n- It creates urgency or fear.\n- It asks for information the real organisation already has.\n- It contains a link to a website you do not recognise, or a phone number you did not previously know.","## What to do","- Do not tap the link or reply, not even with STOP, which can confirm that your number is active.\n- Contact the organisation through its official app or website, or by calling the number printed on your card or bill.\n- Report the message. Many countries have a number to forward scam texts to, and phones allow you to report spam.\n- Block the sender and delete the message.","## If you did tap the link","If you entered a password, change it immediately. If you entered card details, contact your bank to block the card. If you installed something, remove it and run a security scan. Watch your accounts for unusual activity over the following weeks.","## Key takeaway","A text message is not more trustworthy than an email. Never act on a link inside an unexpected message: go to the official app or site yourself."],"quiz":null,"cat":2},
 {"id":"social-media-scams","t":"Social media scams: fake friends, fake offers and fake emergencies","r":"3 min read","tag":"Phishing","date":"October 2026","paras":["Social media makes it easy for scammers to reach millions of people, learn about their lives and build trust. Many scams here do not use malware at all: they rely on conversation and on the assumption that a profile with a friendly photo is a real person.","## Common scams","- Hijacked friend accounts: a message from a friend asks for money urgently, a code or a vote link. The friend's account was taken over earlier, and the attacker now uses their trusted name.\n- Giveaways and prizes: you must share a post, pay a small fee or enter details to claim a reward.\n- Fake investment and trading tips: promises of fast, guaranteed profits from someone who just messaged you.\n- Romance and friendship scams: an attractive stranger builds a relationship and eventually asks for money or personal photos.\n- Fake support accounts: when you complain publicly to a company, a lookalike account sends you a link to fix the problem.\n- Impersonation: a copy of your own profile asks your friends to add the new account.","## Red flags","The messages create urgency, ask for money or codes, move the conversation to another app, refuse a video call, or use payment methods that cannot be reversed such as gift cards, cryptocurrency and bank transfers to strangers.","## How to protect yourself","- Turn on two-factor authentication on every social account.\n- Check unusual messages from friends by contacting them another way.\n- Keep your profile and friend list private enough that strangers cannot easily copy your information.\n- Never send money, codes or personal documents to someone you have not met and verified.\n- Reverse search profile photos to see whether they belong to someone else.\n- Be sceptical of any offer that seems too good to be true.","## If you are targeted","Block and report the account, stop all contact and do not pay. If you already shared money or details, contact your bank or the relevant service quickly, change your passwords and warn your friends if your own account was affected.","## Key takeaway","On social media a familiar name or a friendly photo is not proof of identity. Verify unusual requests through another channel, and never pay or share codes because of a message."],"quiz":null,"cat":2},
 {"id":"quishing","t":"Quishing: when a QR code is the trap","r":"3 min read","tag":"Phishing","date":"October 2026","paras":["Quishing is phishing that uses QR codes. The code hides a web address, so you cannot see where it leads until you scan it. That makes it a handy way for attackers to send people to a fake login page or a malicious download.","## Where fake QR codes appear","- Emails or posters that ask you to scan a code to verify your account, claim a prize or view a document.\n- Stickers placed over the real code on parking meters, restaurant tables, posters or payment terminals.\n- Unexpected letters or parcels that include a code.\n- Fake job adverts and event flyers.","## Why it works","Security filters can read links in the text of an email but often cannot read an address hidden in an image. Scanning with a phone also moves the attack from a protected work computer to a personal phone. The small screen makes it harder to read the full address before the page opens.","## How to stay safe","- Treat a QR code like a link in a message: only scan it if you expect it and trust the source.\n- After scanning, read the address that appears before you open it. Look for the correct domain and watch for lookalike spellings.\n- Be suspicious of a sticker that looks like it has been placed on top of another code.\n- Never enter a password, a verification code or card details on a page opened from a code unless you are sure it is genuine.\n- For payments and accounts, use the official app or type the address yourself instead.\n- Keep your phone updated and use a scanner that shows the link before opening it.","## If you scanned a suspicious code","Close the page without entering anything. If you did enter details, change the password or contact your bank, and run a security scan if you installed something.","## Key takeaway","A QR code is just a link in a different shape. Check where it goes before you trust it, and never use one to log in or pay unless you are certain where it came from."],"quiz":null,"cat":2},
 {"id":"spear-phishing","t":"Spear phishing: scams written for you","r":"3 min read","tag":"Phishing","date":"October 2026","paras":["Regular phishing is sent to thousands of people in the hope that a few will respond. Spear phishing is aimed at one person or a small group, and the message is customised using real information about them. The name comes from a fisherman choosing a single target with a spear instead of casting a wide net.","## How attackers prepare","Attackers collect details from public sources: social media, school or company websites, conference programmes, job adverts and earlier data leaks. They learn your name, role, colleagues, interests and recent activities. They then write a message that looks like part of your normal life, such as a note from your manager about a document, a message from a teacher about an assignment or a message from a supplier about an invoice.","## What makes it dangerous","The message may use the real names of people you work with, refer to a real project or event, and arrive at a believable time. The usual warning signs, such as generic greetings and poor spelling, may be missing. The goal can be credentials, money, access to a network or sensitive files.","## Who is targeted","Anyone can be, but attackers prefer people with access: administrators, finance staff, students with accounts at universities, journalists, and people who handle sensitive information. A senior target is sometimes called whaling.","## How to defend yourself","- Limit what you share publicly, such as your role, your contact details and your schedule.\n- Verify unexpected requests, particularly about money, passwords or documents, using a separate channel.\n- Treat urgency and secrecy as warnings, even when the sender looks familiar.\n- Check sender addresses and link destinations carefully.\n- Use two-factor authentication, preferably a security key, so that a stolen password is not enough.\n- Report suspicious messages so that others in your organisation are warned.","## Key takeaway","The more personal a message feels, the more important it is to check it. Familiar names and accurate details do not prove that a message is genuine."],"quiz":null,"cat":2},
 {"id":"vishing","t":"Vishing: voice phishing and scam phone calls","r":"3 min read","tag":"Phishing","date":"October 2026","paras":["Vishing is phishing by voice: a phone call or voice message in which a scammer pretends to be someone official in order to obtain money, codes or personal information. A live voice makes it feel more trustworthy and lets the caller react to your doubts.","## Typical scripts","- Bank or card fraud team: your account has been compromised, move your money to a safe account or read out the code we just sent you.\n- Tech support: your computer has a virus, install remote access software so we can fix it.\n- Government or tax authority: you owe money or face legal action, and must pay immediately, often by gift cards or transfer.\n- Delivery or utilities: a problem with an order or a bill needs an immediate payment.\n- Family emergency: someone claims to be a relative in trouble, and sometimes uses a cloned voice.","## The tricks they use","Callers can fake the number shown on your screen, so it may appear to come from your bank. They create urgency and fear, keep you on the line so you cannot check, and often ask you to read out one-time codes, which are the codes that approve a payment or reset a password.","## How to respond","- Remember that a real bank never asks you to move money to a safe account or to share a full PIN, password or verification code.\n- Hang up and call back using the number on your card, bill or official website, preferably from a different phone or after waiting a few minutes so that the line is clear.\n- Never install software because a caller asks you to.\n- Do not trust the caller ID.\n- Agree a family code word for emergencies, and check by calling the relative directly.\n- If you feel pressure to decide immediately, that pressure is itself the warning sign.","## If you were fooled","Contact your bank straight away to block cards and reverse payments if possible, change affected passwords, and report the call to the authorities in your country. Speed matters.","## Key takeaway","A caller who creates urgency is giving you the best reason to hang up. Verify using a number you find yourself, and never share codes."],"quiz":null,"cat":2},
 {"id":"business-email-compromise","t":"Business email compromise: fake emails that move real money","r":"3 min read","tag":"Phishing","date":"October 2026","paras":["Business email compromise, often shortened to BEC, is a scam in which an attacker poses as a boss, a supplier or a trusted partner by email in order to get someone to send money or sensitive information. It usually contains no malware and no suspicious attachment, which is why it can slip past security filters.","## How it happens","There are two main routes. In one, the attacker spoofs or imitates an address, for example by registering a domain that differs from the real one by a single letter. In the other, they break into a real mailbox and send messages from the genuine account, sometimes after studying earlier conversations so that their reply fits perfectly.","## Typical scenarios","- The fake boss: a message from the chief executive or a manager asks an employee to buy gift cards or pay an urgent invoice, and to keep it confidential.\n- The changed bank details: a supplier appears to announce a new bank account for future payments.\n- The payroll switch: an employee seems to ask the human resources team to send their salary to a new account.\n- The invoice interception: an attacker alters a genuine invoice thread so that the payment goes to their account.","## Why it works","It relies on authority, urgency and routine. Staff are used to following instructions from senior people and paying invoices, so a request that looks normal and carries pressure is acted on before anyone checks.","## Defences for organisations and individuals","- Confirm any payment request or change of bank details by phoning a known number, not by replying to the email.\n- Use a second approval for payments and for changes to payment details.\n- Treat secrecy and extreme urgency as warning signs, even when the message appears to come from a senior person.\n- Check the actual sender domain and look for small spelling changes.\n- Protect email accounts with two-factor authentication, so attackers cannot take over genuine mailboxes.\n- Report incidents immediately: banks can sometimes recall a payment if they are told quickly.","## Relevance for students","You may meet this form of fraud when paying fees, rent or deposits, or when working part-time. Always confirm new payment details directly with the organisation.","## Key takeaway","Money requests need verification outside the email thread. A short phone call to a number you already know can stop the whole scam."],"quiz":null,"cat":2},
 {"id":"computer-viruses","t":"Computer viruses: what they are and how they spread","r":"3 min read","tag":"Malware","date":"October 2026","paras":["A computer virus is a program that attaches itself to a legitimate file or program and spreads when that file is run or shared. Like a biological virus, it needs a host and a trigger: it does nothing until someone opens the infected item.","## What makes it a virus","The defining feature is that it copies itself into other files, such as documents, programs or the boot area of a drive. When an infected file is opened or copied to another computer, the virus runs and infects more files. This is different from a worm, which spreads on its own across networks, and from a trojan, which pretends to be something useful.","## How viruses spread today","- Email attachments and downloads from unofficial sites.\n- Infected USB drives and external disks.\n- Pirated software, cracks and key generators.\n- Documents that ask you to enable macros.\n- Shared files on infected computers.\nIn the early days, viruses travelled on floppy disks. Today most malware uses different techniques, but the basic rule remains: running unknown code is the risk.","## What a virus can do","Some viruses are harmless pranks, but many damage or delete files, slow the system, steal data or open the door to other malware. A virus may also hide itself, so you may not notice anything for a while.","## Signs of infection","- The computer becomes unusually slow or crashes.\n- Programs open by themselves or files disappear or change.\n- Strange pop-ups, new toolbars or changed browser settings.\n- Security software is switched off and cannot be turned back on.\n- High disk or network activity when you are not using the machine.","## How to prevent infection","- Install software only from official sources.\n- Do not open attachments you did not expect, even from people you know.\n- Keep your operating system and applications updated.\n- Use up-to-date security software and run regular scans.\n- Disable macros in documents unless you trust the source.\n- Back up your files so that you can recover if something goes wrong.","## If you suspect a virus","Disconnect the computer from the network, run a full scan with trusted security software, and change important passwords from a clean device. If the problem persists, restore from a backup or reinstall the system.","## Key takeaway","A virus needs you to run something. Careful downloads, updates and backups stop most of them."],"quiz":null,"cat":3},
 {"id":"trojans","t":"Trojans: malware disguised as something you want","r":"3 min read","tag":"Malware","date":"October 2026","paras":["The name comes from the ancient story of the wooden horse: a gift that hid soldiers inside. A trojan is malware that pretends to be a useful or harmless program, so that you install it yourself. Unlike a virus, it does not spread by copying itself. It relies on deception.","## How trojans reach you","- Cracked or pirated software and game cheats.\n- Free tools, fake updates or fake antivirus programs.\n- Apps from unofficial stores, or lookalike apps that copy a popular name.\n- Email attachments disguised as invoices, documents or photos.\n- Links on websites telling you that your video player or browser needs updating.","## What they do once installed","A trojan opens a door for the attacker. Depending on the type it may steal passwords and banking details, install further malware such as ransomware, give remote control of the device, record the screen or microphone, or make the device part of a botnet used to attack others. Banking trojans specifically target online banking sessions.","## Why people fall for them","A trojan offers something attractive: free software, a shortcut or a necessary fix. The installer may even do part of what it promises. Users also tend to approve permission prompts quickly because they want the program to work.","## How to avoid trojans","- Download software only from official websites and app stores.\n- Avoid pirated programs, game cheats and cracks, which are among the most common carriers.\n- Be suspicious of pop-ups that tell you to update or to remove a virus.\n- Check file extensions: a file named photo.jpg.exe is a program, not a picture.\n- Do not open unexpected attachments.\n- Keep security software and your system updated, and stay alert to permission requests that do not match what the program does.","## If you installed something suspicious","Disconnect from the internet, uninstall the program, run a full scan and change passwords from a different, clean device. On a phone, remove apps you do not recognise and check which ones have administrator access.","## Key takeaway","A trojan only works if you let it in. Source matters more than appearance: install only from official places, and treat free offers with caution."],"quiz":null,"cat":3},
 {"id":"computer-worms","t":"Computer worms: malware that spreads by itself","r":"3 min read","tag":"Malware","date":"October 2026","paras":["A worm is a type of malware that copies itself from computer to computer across a network without needing anyone to open a file. Because it spreads automatically, a worm can infect huge numbers of machines in a short time.","## How worms differ from viruses","A virus needs a host file and some action from a user. A worm is a stand-alone program that finds new targets on its own, usually by exploiting a vulnerability in software or by sending itself to the contacts it finds in an email account.","## Famous examples","- The Morris worm in 1988 was one of the first to spread widely over the early internet and slowed down many connected computers.\n- The ILOVEYOU worm in 2000 spread by email, with a message that tempted people to open an attachment.\n- WannaCry in 2017 spread across networks by exploiting a flaw in Windows file sharing and locked files for ransom. A fix for the flaw had already been released, so computers that had been updated were protected.","## What worms can do","The spreading itself can overload networks and mail servers. Many worms also carry a payload: installing backdoors, stealing information, deleting files, or locking data for ransom.","## How to protect yourself","- Install updates promptly. Worms often use vulnerabilities for which a patch is already available.\n- Turn on automatic updates for your operating system and key programs.\n- Use a firewall, which blocks unwanted incoming connections.\n- Do not open unexpected email attachments or links, even if they appear to come from a contact.\n- Disable services you do not use, such as file sharing on public networks.\n- Keep security software active.","## If a worm gets in","Disconnect the device from the network at once so that it cannot spread further, run a full scan, and update the system. In an organisation, report it immediately so the affected machines can be isolated.","## Key takeaway","Worms spread without help, so protection is mostly about fixing vulnerabilities early. Updates and firewalls are your best defence."],"quiz":null,"cat":3},
 {"id":"spyware","t":"Spyware: software that watches you","r":"3 min read","tag":"Malware","date":"October 2026","paras":["Spyware is software that secretly monitors what you do and sends the information to someone else. It may collect browsing habits, messages, passwords, location, files, or even recordings from the camera and microphone, often without any visible sign.","## Types of spyware","- Information stealers that collect saved passwords, cookies and card details.\n- Keyloggers that record what you type.\n- Screen and audio recorders.\n- Tracking software that follows location and activity.\n- Stalkerware, installed by someone close to you on your phone to monitor you in secret.\n- Commercial spyware, which is advanced software sold to governments and used against a very small number of high-risk targets such as journalists and activists.","## How it gets on a device","Through trojans and fake apps, infected downloads, malicious email attachments, bundled software, and physical access, for instance when someone installs a monitoring app on your unlocked phone.","## Signs to watch for","- Battery draining quickly or the device becoming hot when idle.\n- Unexpectedly high data usage.\n- Unknown apps, or apps with unusual permissions or administrator rights.\n- Camera or microphone indicators turning on unexpectedly.\n- Settings that change by themselves, or security software that cannot be opened.\nThese signs can have innocent causes, so they are clues rather than proof.","## How to protect yourself","- Use a strong screen lock and never leave your phone unlocked with others.\n- Install apps only from official stores and review permissions regularly.\n- Keep the system and apps updated.\n- Avoid suspicious links, attachments and cracked software.\n- Use reputable security software and run scans.","## If you find spyware","Remove the suspicious app or run a scan, change your passwords from a different, clean device, and consider a factory reset if the problem persists. If you suspect that someone you know installed it, think about your safety before acting, because removing it could alert them. Seek help from a trusted person or a support organisation.","## Key takeaway","Spyware works in silence. Control who has access to your device, install only trusted apps, and check permissions from time to time."],"quiz":null,"cat":3},
 {"id":"keyloggers","t":"Keyloggers: when someone records what you type","r":"3 min read","tag":"Malware","date":"October 2026","paras":["A keylogger records the keys you press, so that an attacker or snooper can read your passwords, messages and searches. Some are software and some are small hardware devices, and many are designed to stay hidden.","## Software keyloggers","These run on your computer or phone, often as part of a trojan or spyware package. They log keystrokes, and some also capture screenshots, clipboard contents and the names of the windows you use. The collected data is sent to the attacker over the internet.","## Hardware keyloggers","A small device is placed between the keyboard cable and the computer or built into a keyboard. It needs physical access, which makes shared computers in libraries, internet cafes and schools a particular risk.","## Legitimate uses and misuse","Employers sometimes monitor company machines, and parents may use monitoring tools on children's devices, but these should be open and lawful. Secretly installing a keylogger on someone else's device to spy on them is a form of abuse and illegal in many countries.","## Signs of a keylogger","Typing may lag, there may be unknown programs or processes, or accounts may show logins that you did not make. But many keyloggers show no symptoms at all, so prevention matters more than detection.","## How to protect yourself","- Keep your system, browser and security software updated, and avoid pirated software and unknown attachments.\n- Use two-factor authentication, so a password captured by a keylogger is not enough.\n- Use a password manager to fill in credentials, since this avoids typing them.\n- Avoid logging in to important accounts on shared or public computers. If you must, log out and do not allow the browser to save passwords.\n- Check the cables behind computers you use regularly.\n- Use on-screen keyboards or a different device for sensitive actions if you suspect a problem.","## If you suspect one","Run a full security scan on the device, and change your passwords and recovery details from a different, clean device. Review account activity, and consider reinstalling the system if you cannot find the cause.","## Key takeaway","A keylogger can reveal everything you type. Two-factor authentication and a password manager reduce the damage if one is present."],"quiz":null,"cat":3},
 {"id":"adware","t":"Adware: annoying, and sometimes dangerous","r":"3 min read","tag":"Malware","date":"October 2026","paras":["Adware is software that displays unwanted advertising. In its mildest form, it is an irritation: pop-ups, banners and redirects. In its worst forms it tracks your browsing, slows your device and opens the way to more serious malware.","## Legitimate ads and adware","Many free apps display ads openly, and that is how they make money. Adware is different because it appears without your clear permission, often bundled with other software, and is hard to remove. It may change your browser homepage or search engine, add toolbars, and insert adverts into pages you visit.","## How adware gets onto devices","- Free software installers that include extra programs, hidden in the small print or in pre-ticked boxes.\n- Cracked programs and download sites that wrap files in their own installers.\n- Fake update prompts and fake antivirus alerts.\n- Browser extensions that look useful.\n- Unofficial apps on phones.","## Why it matters","Besides being annoying, adware can collect data about your browsing and sell it, slow your computer, drain your battery and data allowance, and direct you to malicious pages. Some adware downloads other malware.","## Signs of adware","- Pop-ups appear even when no browser is open.\n- The homepage or search engine changes by itself.\n- New toolbars, extensions or apps that you do not recall installing.\n- Pages that you visit contain extra adverts.\n- The device is slow or the battery runs out quickly.","## How to avoid and remove it","- Download from official sources, and choose custom or advanced installation to untick extras.\n- Read what you are agreeing to, and refuse notifications from websites you do not need.\n- Remove unknown extensions and programs, and reset browser settings if needed.\n- Run a scan with reputable security software.\n- Never click a pop-up claiming that your device is infected.\n- On phones, check which apps may display over other apps, and remove those you do not need.","## Key takeaway","Free software sometimes costs more than it seems. Install from trusted sources, read the options, and clean up anything you did not choose."],"quiz":null,"cat":3},
 {"id":"antivirus-and-updates","t":"Antivirus and updates: your everyday defence","r":"3 min read","tag":"Malware","date":"October 2026","paras":["Two habits protect more people from more attacks than almost anything else: keeping software updated, and using reputable security software. Neither is exciting, but both close the doors that attackers rely on.","## Why updates matter","Software contains mistakes, and some of them are security vulnerabilities. When researchers or the vendor find one, they release an update to fix it. Once the fix is public, attackers study it and build tools against people who have not yet installed it. Large outbreaks, including the WannaCry attack in 2017, spread through a flaw for which a patch already existed. Updating promptly is therefore one of the best defences you have.","## What to update","- Your operating system: Windows, macOS, Android, iOS or Linux.\n- Your web browser and its extensions.\n- Apps and programs, especially those that open files from the internet.\n- Router firmware, which is easy to forget.\n- Security software itself.\nTurn on automatic updates where you can. Restart when asked, because many updates only take effect after a restart. Stop using software that is no longer supported and no longer receives security updates.","## What antivirus does","Security software compares files and behaviour with known malicious patterns, watches for suspicious actions and can block dangerous websites. Modern systems include built-in protection, such as Microsoft Defender on Windows, which is adequate for many people. A well-known third-party product can add features such as extra web protection.","## What it cannot do","Antivirus does not stop you from giving your password to a fake website, and new threats may not be recognised immediately. It is one layer, not a guarantee.","## Good practice","- Use one reputable security product at a time, as several can conflict.\n- Keep it updated and enabled, and run a full scan occasionally.\n- Get software from official sources, and be wary of free antivirus advertised in pop-ups.\n- Combine it with backups, strong passwords and two-factor authentication.","## Key takeaway","Turn on automatic updates and keep reputable security software running. These small habits stop a large share of common attacks."],"quiz":null,"cat":3},
 {"id":"https-explained","t":"HTTPS: what the padlock really means","r":"3 min read","tag":"Internet security","date":"October 2026","paras":["When you visit a website, your browser and the site exchange information. HTTPS is the secure version of the protocol used for that exchange. The S stands for secure, and it means the connection is encrypted and the site has proven its identity with a certificate.","## What HTTPS protects","- Privacy: other people on the network cannot read the data you send or receive, such as passwords and form contents.\n- Integrity: the data cannot be altered on the way without detection, so nobody can silently insert adverts or malware into a page.\n- Authentication: your browser checks that the certificate matches the domain, which helps ensure that you are connected to the site you typed and not an imitation of it.","## How it works, in brief","When you connect, the server presents a digital certificate issued by a trusted certificate authority. Your browser checks that it is valid and matches the domain. Then the two sides use public-key cryptography to agree on a secret key, and use that key to encrypt everything else for the rest of the session.","## What the padlock does not mean","A padlock does not prove that a website is honest or safe. Criminals can obtain certificates for fake domains, so a phishing site can have HTTPS too. It only says that the connection to that exact domain is encrypted. You still need to check the domain name itself.","## What to look for","- The address begins with https, and the domain is spelled exactly as you expect.\n- The browser shows no warning. If it warns about an invalid or expired certificate, stop and do not click through.\n- Be careful with any site that asks for passwords or card details over plain http.\n- Many browsers now mark plain http sites as not secure, which is a useful signal.","## HTTPS everywhere","Most major sites now use HTTPS by default, and browsers can be set to use it whenever it is available. It does not replace other defences, but it removes a large class of simple attacks on public networks.","## Key takeaway","HTTPS means your connection is private and goes to the domain shown. Always check the domain too, because the padlock alone does not tell you whether the site is trustworthy."],"quiz":null,"cat":4},
 {"id":"cookies","t":"Cookies: small files that remember you","r":"3 min read","tag":"Internet security","date":"October 2026","paras":["A cookie is a small piece of data that a website stores in your browser. It lets the site remember something between visits or between pages. Without cookies, you would have to log in again every time you click a link.","## What cookies are used for","- Sessions: keeping you logged in, and keeping items in your basket.\n- Preferences: language, region and display settings.\n- Analytics: counting visitors and seeing how pages are used.\n- Advertising and tracking: following you across different websites to build a profile of your interests.","## First-party and third-party cookies","First-party cookies are set by the site you are visiting and are usually needed for it to work properly. Third-party cookies are set by other domains, such as advertising networks, whose content is embedded in the page. Because the same third party appears on many sites, it can follow you across them. Many browsers now block third-party cookies or limit tracking by default.","## Where the risks lie","- Privacy: tracking cookies can reveal your habits, interests and location to advertisers and data brokers.\n- Session theft: if an attacker steals the cookie that keeps you logged in, they may be able to enter your account without a password. This can happen through malware or on an insecure connection.\n- Persistent identifiers that survive when you think you have cleared your tracks.","## Managing cookies","- When a site asks for consent, choose to reject non-essential cookies when you can.\n- Check your browser settings: block third-party cookies, and clear cookies and site data from time to time.\n- Log out of important accounts on shared computers.\n- Use private browsing on a shared device, remembering that it only clears data stored locally and does not make you invisible online.\n- Consider tracker-blocking extensions from reputable developers.\n- Keep your browser updated, because it contains protections against cookie abuse.","## Cookies are not viruses","Cookies themselves are only data. They do not run programs or install malware. The concern is what they reveal and how they can be misused.","## Key takeaway","Cookies make the web convenient, but tracking cookies build a record of what you do. Reject what you do not need, clear them regularly and protect the ones that keep you signed in."],"quiz":null,"cat":4},
 {"id":"browser-security-settings","t":"Browser security settings: five minutes that make a difference","r":"3 min read","tag":"Internet security","date":"October 2026","paras":["Your web browser is the program you use most to reach the internet, and its default settings are not always the safest. A few changes protect you from many common attacks.","## Keep it updated","Browsers are updated often to fix security flaws. Enable automatic updates and restart when prompted. An outdated browser is one of the easiest ways for malicious sites to attack you.","## Settings worth checking","- Safe browsing or enhanced protection: this warns you about dangerous websites and downloads.\n- HTTPS-only mode: this tries to connect securely and warns you before opening plain http pages.\n- Third-party cookies and tracking: block third-party cookies and turn on tracking protection.\n- Site permissions: review which sites may use your camera, microphone, location and notifications, and remove those you do not recognise. Notification permissions are often abused for spam and scams.\n- Pop-ups and redirects: block them by default.\n- Saved passwords and autofill: if you use a password manager, switch off the browser's own saving or protect it with a strong device password. Do not store card details on shared computers.\n- Downloads: set the browser to ask where to save, and be careful with executable files.","## Extensions","Extensions can read what you do on websites, so install only a few from reputable developers and official stores. Check the permissions and reviews, and remove extensions that you no longer use. Malicious extensions have been caught stealing data or injecting adverts.","## Profiles and sync","Use separate profiles for school, work and personal browsing if you can. If you sync your data to your account, protect that account with a strong password and two-factor authentication, because it unlocks your saved information on every device.","## Shared computers","Use a guest or private window, do not save passwords, log out when you finish and close the browser. Remember to clear the downloads.","## Key takeaway","Update your browser, turn on its protections, trim permissions and extensions, and secure the account that syncs your data. This takes minutes and makes a lasting difference."],"quiz":null,"cat":4},
 {"id":"firewalls","t":"Firewalls: the gatekeeper for your network","r":"3 min read","tag":"Internet security","date":"October 2026","paras":["A firewall is a security system that monitors the traffic going in and out of a device or network and decides whether to allow or block it according to rules. It works like a gatekeeper that checks every visitor against a list.","## Types of firewall","- Software firewalls run on your computer or phone. Windows, macOS and most Linux systems include one, and it is usually switched on by default.\n- Hardware or network firewalls protect a whole network, such as a school, a company or the router in your home, which commonly includes basic firewall features.\n- Next-generation firewalls inspect traffic in more detail, for example identifying applications and known attack patterns.","## What a firewall can do","- Block unsolicited incoming connections, which stops many automated scans and attacks, including those used by worms.\n- Restrict which programs can connect to the internet.\n- Log connection attempts, which helps in spotting unusual activity.\n- Separate parts of a network, so that a problem in one area does not spread to others.","## What it cannot do","A firewall does not understand every kind of trick. It will not stop you from downloading malware, entering your password on a fake site or approving a connection that you should not. If a user or program inside the network starts the connection, the firewall may allow it. It is one layer in a wider set of defences.","## Good practice at home","- Keep the firewall switched on in your operating system.\n- Make sure your router firewall is enabled, and change the default administrator password.\n- Keep router firmware updated and turn off remote administration if you do not need it.\n- Disable features you do not use, such as unnecessary port forwarding.\n- When the system asks whether a program may access the network, allow only what you recognise, and choose the more restricted network profile on public networks.\n- Do not turn the firewall off to make something work, but investigate the proper rule instead.","## Key takeaway","Firewalls block unwanted connections before they reach your device. Keep yours on, keep your router updated, and remember that a firewall cannot protect you from your own clicks."],"quiz":null,"cat":4},
 {"id":"dns-abuse","t":"DNS: the internet's phone book and how it can be abused","r":"3 min read","tag":"Internet security","date":"October 2026","paras":["People remember names such as example.com, but computers need numeric addresses. The Domain Name System, or DNS, is the service that translates names into addresses, a bit like a phone book for the internet. Almost every connection you make starts with a DNS lookup.","## How it works in outline","When you type a domain name, your device asks a DNS resolver, usually run by your internet provider or a public service, for the matching address. The resolver finds the answer, either from memory or by asking other servers, and returns it. Your browser then connects to that address.","## How DNS gets abused","- DNS spoofing or cache poisoning: false answers are inserted so that a real domain name leads to the attacker's server, where a fake site collects passwords.\n- Malicious DNS settings: malware or a compromised router changes your DNS servers to ones the attacker controls, redirecting you silently.\n- Rogue Wi-Fi: a hotspot may hand out its own DNS settings and send you to fake pages.\n- Lookalike domains: attackers register names that differ by one letter from a real brand, relying on you not noticing the difference.\n- Domain hijacking: criminals take over the account that controls a domain and change its records.\n- DNS tunnelling and denial of service attacks that misuse DNS servers to hide data or flood a target.","## Protections","- Secure resolvers: choose a reputable DNS service that blocks known malicious domains, and consider encrypted DNS (DNS over HTTPS or DNS over TLS) so others on the network cannot read or change your lookups.\n- DNSSEC adds digital signatures to DNS answers so that false ones can be detected, although it is not used everywhere.\n- HTTPS still protects you: a fake server cannot present a valid certificate for the real domain, and the browser warns you.\n- Check router settings and change the default administrator password, so nobody can alter your DNS.\n- Look at the address bar carefully, since a fake site will show a different domain.","## If you suspect tampering","Check your device and router DNS settings against those you expect, run a security scan, update your router and change the administrator password.","## Key takeaway","DNS quietly decides where you end up. Use a trusted resolver, secure your router and rely on HTTPS warnings and careful reading of the address bar."],"quiz":null,"cat":4},
 {"id":"pretexting","t":"Pretexting: scams built on a believable story","r":"3 min read","tag":"Social engineering","date":"October 2026","paras":["Pretexting is a social engineering technique in which an attacker invents a believable scenario, the pretext, to persuade you to give information or take an action. Instead of tricking you with a fake link, they trick you with a story and a role, such as a bank officer, a new colleague, a researcher or a support agent.","## How it works","The attacker usually researches you first, using social media, public records and earlier leaks, so that they can sound informed. They then contact you with a plausible reason: they need to verify your identity, fix a problem with your account, complete a survey or deliver something. Small details they already know, such as your name or school, make them seem genuine, and they use this credibility to ask for more.","## Common pretexts","- Support staff who need your password or a verification code to fix a problem.\n- A manager, teacher or official asking for urgent information.\n- A new employee, student or contractor who needs access or a document.\n- A survey, prize draw or research project that asks for personal details.\n- A caller doing a security check on your bank account.\n- Someone claiming to be a family member or friend who has lost their phone.","## Why it works","People are helpful, respect authority and want to resolve problems. Pretexts use those instincts. They also use time pressure, so you do not stop to think.","## Warning signs","- The contact is unexpected and asks for sensitive information.\n- The person cannot or will not be verified through an official channel.\n- You are pushed to act immediately or keep the conversation secret.\n- The request does not match the way the real organisation works. For example, no genuine bank asks for your full password.","## How to respond","- Pause. Ask who they are and why they need the information.\n- Verify independently: end the conversation and contact the organisation or person using details you already trust.\n- Give out only the minimum information, and never share passwords or codes.\n- Tell someone, and report the attempt to the real organisation.\n- Limit what you publish about yourself, because it provides material for convincing stories.","## Key takeaway","A good story is not proof. When someone you have not verified asks for information, verify them first through a channel that you choose."],"quiz":null,"cat":5},
 {"id":"baiting","t":"Baiting: when the trap looks like a gift","r":"3 min read","tag":"Social engineering","date":"October 2026","paras":["Baiting is a social engineering technique that uses a tempting offer to lure victims into a trap, in the same way that bait is used in fishing. The bait may be something free, something valuable or simply something that arouses curiosity.","## Digital baiting","- Free downloads: films, games, music, cracked software and cheats that contain malware.\n- Fake giveaways and prizes that ask you to log in or install something.\n- Adverts and pop-ups promising quick money or a free phone.\n- Files with attractive names shared in chats, such as a leaked exam or photos.\n- Fake job or scholarship offers that ask you to open an attachment.","## Physical baiting","A well-known example is the infected USB drive left in a place where someone will find it, for example in a car park, a library or on a desk, sometimes labelled with something intriguing like salaries or private. Curious people plug it in, and the drive installs malware or acts as a keyboard that types commands. Charging stations and free cables in public places can also be abused.","## Why it works","Curiosity and the wish to get something for free are strong human drives. Bait lowers your guard, so you act before considering the risk.","## Warning signs","- Something is offered for free that normally costs money.\n- The source is unknown or unofficial.\n- You are asked to disable security, install a program or enter credentials to get the reward.\n- The offer is urgent or limited in time.","## How to protect yourself","- Remember that if something seems too good to be true, it probably is.\n- Download only from official sources.\n- Never plug in a USB drive or memory card of unknown origin. If you find one, give it to the owner or the IT team.\n- Do not use public charging ports for sensitive devices, or use a charge-only cable or your own power bank.\n- Keep software and security tools updated.\n- Think before you scan, click or install, and ask what the sender gains.","## Key takeaway","Bait only works if you take it. Free offers and found devices are common traps, so decline them or hand them to someone who can check."],"quiz":null,"cat":5},
 {"id":"impersonation-scams","t":"Impersonation scams: when someone pretends to be someone else","r":"3 min read","tag":"Social engineering","date":"October 2026","paras":["In an impersonation scam, the criminal pretends to be a person or organisation you know or trust: a friend, a family member, a bank, a government agency, a delivery company or your school. The goal is to gain your trust quickly and then ask for money, information or access.","## Who they pretend to be","- Friends and relatives: a message from a new number claims to be your son, daughter or friend who needs money immediately. Voice cloning software can now make a call sound like someone you know.\n- Banks and payment services, with a warning about fraud.\n- Government, police or tax officials, with threats about fines or arrests.\n- Tech support and delivery companies.\n- Celebrities, influencers or companies offering prizes and investment opportunities.\n- Your boss or teacher, asking for a quick favour.","## How they do it","They can spoof email addresses and phone numbers, copy logos and names, and create profiles that look real. They may take over a genuine account and use it to contact the real person's friends. Combined with information found online, this makes them convincing.","## Red flags","- A change of number or account for someone you know.\n- A request for money, gift cards, cryptocurrency or personal documents.\n- Pressure to act immediately and to keep it secret.\n- A refusal to take a video call or to answer a personal question.\n- A threat of arrest, legal action or lost access.","## How to protect yourself","- Check through a different channel: call the person on the number you already have, or visit the official website or app.\n- Agree a family code word for emergencies.\n- Ask a question only the real person could answer, but remember that scammers research.\n- Do not trust caller ID or display names.\n- Never send money, codes or documents based on a message alone.\n- Report impersonation to the platform and, for fraud, to the police.","## If you have paid","Contact your bank immediately, report it to the authorities and keep all messages as evidence. Acting quickly may allow a payment to be stopped.","## Key takeaway","A familiar name, voice or logo can be copied. Verify any unusual request independently before you act."],"quiz":null,"cat":5},
 {"id":"social-manipulation","t":"Social manipulation: the psychology behind scams","r":"3 min read","tag":"Social engineering","date":"October 2026","paras":["Most scams work not because of clever technology but because of predictable human psychology. Social manipulation is the use of those natural tendencies to steer people into decisions they would not otherwise make. Knowing the techniques makes it easier to notice them.","## Common levers","- Authority: we tend to obey people who seem to be in charge, such as officials, managers or technical experts.\n- Urgency and scarcity: limited time or limited supply makes us act before thinking.\n- Fear: threats of fines, lost accounts or arrest cause panic, and panic shuts down careful reasoning.\n- Liking and familiarity: we trust people who are friendly, similar to us or who pay us compliments.\n- Reciprocity: if someone does us a small favour, we feel we owe them something.\n- Social proof: if others seem to be doing it, we assume it is safe. Fake reviews and followers use this.\n- Commitment: once we have agreed to a small request, we are more likely to agree to larger ones.\n- Curiosity and greed: prizes, secrets and shocking news encourage us to click.","## Where you see it","Phishing emails, fake giveaways, romance scams, investment schemes and calls from fake support agents all combine several of these levers. The message might claim to come from a senior person, set a deadline, threaten a penalty and offer a simple way out.","## Why intelligent people fall for it","It has nothing to do with intelligence. Anyone who is busy, tired, worried or distracted is more vulnerable. Emotional pressure is designed to reduce careful thought.","## How to defend against manipulation","- Notice your own feelings. If a message makes you anxious, excited or rushed, treat that as a warning.\n- Slow down: wait, take a break, and sleep on any big decision.\n- Verify through another channel.\n- Ask what the sender wants and who benefits.\n- Talk to a trusted person. A second opinion breaks the spell.\n- Learn the patterns, so that you recognise them when they are used on you.","## Key takeaway","Scammers aim at your emotions, not your devices. When you feel rushed or pressured, stop and check before doing anything."],"quiz":null,"cat":5},
 {"id":"tailgating","t":"Tailgating: following someone through a locked door","r":"3 min read","tag":"Social engineering","date":"October 2026","paras":["Tailgating, also called piggybacking, is a physical social engineering technique in which an unauthorised person follows an authorised one through a secured entrance. It needs no technical skill, only politeness and good timing.","## How it happens","The attacker waits near a door that needs a card or code, and walks in behind someone who has just opened it. They may carry boxes, a coffee or a laptop and ask someone to hold the door, wear a uniform or a lanyard, pretend to be a delivery person or a contractor, or simply look as though they belong. Most people do not want to appear rude, so they let them in.","## Why it matters","Once inside, an attacker can reach computers, documents, network sockets and unlocked devices. They may plant a device, steal equipment, read unattended screens or use an empty desk to connect to the network. Physical access can bypass many digital defences.","## Where it applies","- Offices, schools, universities and dormitories.\n- Server rooms and laboratories.\n- Apartment buildings and car parks.\n- Events and conferences where badges are used.","## How to prevent it","- Do not hold secure doors for people you do not know. Politely ask them to use their own card or to check in at reception.\n- Wear and check identification, and challenge or report people without it, in a friendly manner.\n- Close doors behind you and do not prop them open.\n- Do not lend your access card or share codes.\n- Lock your screen and devices when you leave, even briefly.\n- Report suspicious behaviour or missing badges to security or staff, rather than confronting someone alone.","## Handling the awkward moment","A simple line works: I am sorry, could you swipe in yourself, it is the rule here. Genuine staff understand, and real visitors can be directed to reception. Organisations help by making this a clear, shared rule so that no one feels rude.","## Key takeaway","Good manners can be a security gap. Letting a stranger in is a decision, so send visitors to reception and keep secured areas secure."],"quiz":null,"cat":5},
 {"id":"shoulder-surfing","t":"Shoulder surfing: someone is watching your screen","r":"3 min read","tag":"Social engineering","date":"October 2026","paras":["Shoulder surfing means watching someone as they enter a password, PIN or private information, either by looking over their shoulder or from a distance. It needs no hacking, and it happens in public places every day.","## Where it happens","- Cash machines and card terminals where PINs are typed.\n- Trains, buses, planes, cafes and libraries where screens are visible to others.\n- Offices and classrooms with open layouts.\n- Phone unlocking in crowds.\n- Video calls with sensitive information visible behind you.","## Modern variations","Attackers may use a camera, a mobile phone or binoculars to record screens or keypads from a distance. People can also watch your fingers on the screen to learn the unlock pattern, and cameras in public places sometimes capture keypad entry. Notes and passwords on sticky notes near a screen are also visible to others.","## Why it matters","A PIN or password you see being typed is as good as one that has been stolen. If the observer also takes your phone, they have both the lock code and the device.","## How to protect yourself","- Cover the keypad with your hand when entering a PIN, and make sure nobody is close behind you.\n- Be aware of who is nearby before you unlock your phone or enter a password on public transport or in a cafe.\n- Use biometrics such as fingerprint or face recognition where available, which are harder to observe than a typed code.\n- Use a privacy screen filter on laptops that you use in public places.\n- Turn down brightness and angle the screen away from others.\n- Reduce the amount of sensitive work you do in crowded areas.\n- Use a password manager so that you type fewer passwords.\n- Change a PIN or password if you suspect that someone has seen it.","## At home and work","Do not write passwords on notes stuck to a monitor. Lock your screen when you step away.","## Key takeaway","Treat your screen and keypad as private. A few seconds of awareness in public stops one of the simplest forms of attack."],"quiz":null,"cat":5},
 {"id":"app-permissions","t":"App permissions: what you are really agreeing to","r":"3 min read","tag":"Mobile and app security","date":"October 2026","paras":["Every time an app asks to use your camera, contacts or location, it is asking for a permission. These requests are the main control you have over what an app can see and do on your phone. Many people tap allow without reading, but permissions are worth a moment of thought.","## What permissions cover","- Camera and microphone: can capture images, video and sound.\n- Location: can show where you are, sometimes all the time.\n- Contacts and calendar: can read the people and events in your life.\n- Photos and files: can read or change your pictures and documents.\n- Messages and call logs: can read sensitive personal communication.\n- Notifications, Bluetooth and nearby devices.\n- Special access such as accessibility services and device administrator, which can see the screen and control the device, and are often abused by malware.","## Does the app need it?","The key question is whether the permission matches the purpose. A map app needs your location. A torch app does not need your contacts. A photo editor needs access to the photos you choose, not everything. If a request seems excessive, deny it or choose not to install the app.","## Smarter choices","Modern phones let you choose more precisely:\n- While using the app, rather than always.\n- Approximate location, instead of exact location.\n- Selected photos, instead of the whole library.\n- Only this time, which grants permission once.","## Why this matters","Apps can collect data for advertising or sell it to others, and malicious apps can steal information. Even a legitimate app can be compromised or poorly protected. Fewer permissions mean less that can go wrong.","## Regular check-up","- Open your phone settings and view permissions by type, for example which apps can use the microphone.\n- Remove access from apps you no longer use, and uninstall them.\n- Review permissions after major updates, because apps can request more.\n- Be especially careful with accessibility and administrator access, and never grant them to an app that does not clearly need them.\n- Use the privacy dashboard on your phone to see when apps accessed sensors.","## Key takeaway","Grant only what an app needs, and only when needed. A permission you never gave cannot be misused."],"quiz":null,"cat":6},
 {"id":"official-app-stores","t":"Official app stores: why where you download matters","r":"3 min read","tag":"Mobile and app security","date":"October 2026","paras":["Apps are one of the main ways malware reaches phones. Official stores, such as Google Play and the Apple App Store, are not perfect, but they provide a layer of protection that unofficial sources do not.","## What official stores do","- Review apps before and after publication, using automated scanning and human checks.\n- Require developers to follow policies about permissions, privacy and behaviour.\n- Remove harmful apps and, in some cases, disable them on devices that have installed them.\n- Deliver updates through a trusted route.\n- On iPhone, restrict where apps can be installed from, and on Android, include built-in scanning that checks apps.","## Why unofficial sources are riskier","Websites, messaging links and third-party app stores that offer modified or pirated apps often carry malware. A cracked version of a paid game or a free version of a premium tool may include spyware or a trojan. Installing from outside the store also means that you rely on whoever packaged the file.","## Official does not mean perfect","Malicious apps have occasionally passed review, including fake copies of popular programs, apps that behave well at first and turn harmful after an update, and apps that collect more data than they should. Check the app yourself before installing.","## How to check an app","- Make sure that the developer name is the official one, since fake apps copy names and icons.\n- Look at the number of downloads and the reviews, including the recent and the negative ones.\n- Read the permissions and the privacy information.\n- Be sceptical about unknown apps that promise too much, such as free premium features.\n- Get apps from the link on the company's official website.","## Good habits","- Keep the setting that blocks installation from unknown sources turned off.\n- Install updates promptly.\n- Remove apps you do not use.\n- Never follow a link in a message that tells you to install an app outside the store.","## Key takeaway","Install apps only from official stores, and still check the developer, reviews and permissions. A little care prevents most mobile malware."],"quiz":null,"cat":6},
 {"id":"screen-locks-biometrics","t":"Screen locks and biometrics: the front door of your phone","r":"3 min read","tag":"Mobile and app security","date":"October 2026","paras":["Your phone contains messages, photos, banking apps and your email, and it can reset passwords for other services. A screen lock is the first line of defence if the phone is lost or stolen, or if someone simply picks it up.","## Why you need a lock","Without a lock, anyone who finds your phone can open your accounts and read private information. A lock also enables encryption of the phone's storage on modern devices, which protects data even if someone tries to read it directly.","## Choosing a lock","- PIN: use at least six digits, and avoid birth dates, repeated or sequential numbers.\n- Password or passphrase: the strongest option, especially for sensitive devices.\n- Pattern: the weakest choice, because patterns are easy to guess, copy or see from the smudges on a screen.\n- Biometrics: fingerprint and face recognition are convenient and make it harder for someone to watch you type a code. They work alongside a PIN or password that acts as a fallback.","## Strengths and limits of biometrics","Biometrics are convenient and make it practical to use a long code. But a fingerprint or a face is not a secret: it can be used by someone else in some situations, for example if you are asleep or compelled to unlock. You cannot change your fingerprint if it is compromised. Many phones therefore have a quick way to disable biometric unlock temporarily.","## Settings that help","- Short automatic lock time, so the phone locks soon after you stop using it.\n- Hide sensitive notifications on the lock screen.\n- Limit what can be accessed from the lock screen, such as the control panel and voice assistants.\n- Turn on automatic data erasure after many failed attempts, if you accept the risk of accidental erasure.\n- Use a different code from your bank card PIN.\n- Keep a backup of your data.","## Protect the code","Do not tell the code to others and take care when entering it in public places, where someone may see it. If you think someone knows it, change it.","## Key takeaway","Always use a screen lock, ideally a six-digit PIN or longer combined with biometrics, and keep the lock time short."],"quiz":null,"cat":6},
 {"id":"find-my-device","t":"Find My Device: preparing for a lost or stolen phone","r":"3 min read","tag":"Mobile and app security","date":"October 2026","paras":["Losing a phone is more likely than being hacked, and the consequences can be similar: someone may gain access to your accounts and private data. Built-in tools such as Find My on Apple devices and Find My Device on Android let you locate, lock or erase a phone remotely, but only if you set them up in advance.","## What the tools can do","- Show the last known location of the device on a map.\n- Play a sound to help you find it nearby.\n- Lock the device and display a message and contact number on the screen.\n- Erase all data remotely, if recovery is unlikely.\n- On many devices, keep the device locked to your account even after a reset, which makes a stolen phone far less valuable.","## How to set it up","- Turn on the service in your phone's settings under your account name or the security section.\n- Keep location services and an internet connection enabled so that the device can report in.\n- Make sure that you know your Apple ID or Google account password, and protect it with two-factor authentication.\n- Add a trusted contact or a recovery option, and write down your device model and its identification number, known as the IMEI, in a safe place.\n- Try the feature on another device so that you know how it works.","## If you lose your phone","1. Use another device to locate it, and play a sound if it may be nearby.\n2. If you cannot get it back quickly, enable lost mode to lock it.\n3. Contact your mobile operator to block the SIM and report the loss, which prevents calls and codes being intercepted.\n4. Change the passwords of important accounts, starting with email, from a safe device.\n5. Report a theft to the police and your operator, and do not go to meet the thief alone.\n6. Erase the device remotely if recovery is unlikely.","## Common mistakes","Waiting too long, not having an account password to hand and leaving the feature off. Erasing the phone may stop you from tracking it afterwards, so decide based on how sensitive the data is.","## Key takeaway","Set up Find My Device now, while the phone is in your hand. Combined with a screen lock and a backup, it turns a lost phone into a manageable problem."],"quiz":null,"cat":6},
 {"id":"bluetooth-airdrop-risks","t":"Bluetooth and AirDrop risks: wireless features worth managing","r":"3 min read","tag":"Mobile and app security","date":"October 2026","paras":["Bluetooth and similar wireless features, such as AirDrop and Quick Share, are convenient for headphones, car connections and sharing files with people nearby. Leaving them open all the time, however, can expose your device to unwanted contact and, occasionally, attacks.","## What can go wrong","- Unsolicited files and images: strangers in public places send unwanted or offensive content to devices that accept sharing from everyone.\n- Tracking: a device that constantly broadcasts can be recognised and followed by others nearby.\n- Pairing tricks: a malicious device might pretend to be a headset or a speaker so that you connect to it.\n- Vulnerabilities in the Bluetooth software have allowed attackers within range to run code or intercept data, which is why updates matter.\n- Old devices and cheap accessories may use weak pairing without proper security.\n- Fake or spoofed device names in a crowded place.","## Safer settings","- Turn Bluetooth off when you are not using it, or at least avoid leaving it in a discoverable state.\n- For AirDrop or Quick Share, choose contacts only or receiving off, instead of everyone. If you use everyone, set it only for a short time.\n- Accept a transfer only if you know who is sending it and are expecting it.\n- Pair devices at home, not in crowded public places.\n- Remove old paired devices that you no longer use.\n- Keep your phone and your accessories updated.\n- Set a name for your device that does not contain your full name.\n- Do not accept pairing requests that you did not start, and verify the codes shown on both devices.","## Public places","Crowds on public transport, at events and in schools are where unsolicited sharing happens most. A quick switch to contacts-only avoids it.","## Key takeaway","Wireless sharing is useful, but open settings invite trouble. Limit who can send to you and switch features off when you do not need them."],"quiz":null,"cat":6},
 {"id":"mobile-banking-safety","t":"Mobile banking safety: protecting money on your phone","r":"3 min read","tag":"Mobile and app security","date":"October 2026","paras":["Banking apps are generally secure, and they use strong encryption and often stricter protection than websites. Most losses come not from breaking the app but from tricking the user or from an insecure phone. A few habits reduce the risk greatly.","## Protect the phone itself","- Use a strong screen lock and keep the phone updated.\n- Install apps only from official stores, and avoid apps that ask for accessibility or administrator access without good reason, because banking malware abuses them to read the screen and approve transactions.\n- Do not use rooted or jailbroken devices for banking.\n- Set up Find My Device so that you can lock or erase a lost phone.","## Protect the account","- Use the official bank app, downloaded from the link on the bank's own website, or type the address yourself instead of following links in messages.\n- Use a unique, strong password and turn on biometrics and two-factor authentication for the app.\n- Turn on transaction alerts so you notice something wrong immediately, and set sensible limits.\n- Never share your PIN, password, card number with security code, or one-time codes with anyone. Bank staff never ask for them.\n- Check statements regularly.","## Common scams","- Messages or calls pretending to be from the bank, claiming that your account is at risk.\n- Callers who persuade you to install a screen-sharing app, or to move money to a safe account.\n- Fake banking apps or websites.\n- Payments to someone you have not verified, or changes in bank details of a payee.\nIf a message creates urgency, hang up and call the bank on the number printed on your card.","## Safe connections","Avoid banking on public Wi-Fi, or use mobile data or a trusted VPN. Log out when you finish, especially on shared devices.","## If something goes wrong","Contact your bank immediately to block cards and the app, change your passwords, and report the fraud to the authorities. The faster you act, the more likely that money can be recovered.","## Key takeaway","Secure your phone, protect your logins, and never share codes. The bank will never need them."],"quiz":null,"cat":6},
 {"id":"app-review-red-flags","t":"App store reviews and permission red flags: how to judge an app before installing","r":"3 min read","tag":"Mobile and app security","date":"October 2026","paras":["A few minutes of checking before you install an app can save you from malware, privacy problems and scams. The store page and the permission list give you more clues than most people realise.","## Check the developer and the basics","- Who published it? Compare the developer name with the official website of the company. Fake apps often use similar names and icons.\n- How many downloads does it have, and when was it last updated? A popular and regularly updated app is generally safer than one that appeared recently or has been abandoned.\n- Does the description make sense, with clear language and screenshots that match what the app does? Poor grammar and generic text can be warning signs.","## Reading reviews critically","- Look at the distribution of ratings, not only the average.\n- Read the lowest-rated reviews, and the most recent ones. They often mention hidden charges, adverts or data problems.\n- Be wary of many short, similar five-star reviews, which may be fake.\n- Check whether the developer responds to problems.\n- A good rating does not prove safety: malicious apps can have positive reviews.","## Permission red flags","- A simple app that asks for much more than it needs, such as a calculator wanting contacts or a wallpaper app wanting to read messages.\n- Requests for accessibility services or device administrator rights without a clear reason.\n- Requests to display over other apps or to install other apps.\n- Access to SMS, which can be used to intercept codes.\nSee the data safety or privacy section on the store page, which shows what the app collects and shares.","## Other warning signs","- Promises of free premium features, unlimited money in a game, or ways to see who viewed your profile.\n- Pressure to give a rating or to grant permissions at the first opening.\n- Aggressive adverts or subscriptions that are hard to cancel.\n- The same app available only from a link in a message instead of the store.","## After installing","Deny unnecessary permissions, keep the app updated, and uninstall it if it behaves oddly, drains the battery or shows unexpected adverts.","## Key takeaway","Judge an app by the developer, the recent reviews and the permissions it asks for. If the request does not fit the purpose, do not install it."],"quiz":null,"cat":6},
 {"id":"digital-footprint","t":"Your digital footprint: the trail you leave online","r":"3 min read","tag":"Digital privacy and footprint","date":"October 2026","paras":["Every time you use the internet you leave traces. Together, they form your digital footprint: the record of what you post, search, buy, click and share, and what others post about you. Some traces you leave on purpose, and others you leave without noticing.","## Active and passive footprints","- Active: things you deliberately publish, such as posts, photos, comments, profiles and reviews.\n- Passive: data collected about you without you actively sharing it, such as your IP address, location history, browsing habits, device information and the cookies that track your visits.","## Who sees it","Friends, classmates, employers, universities, advertisers, data brokers and sometimes criminals can all see parts of it. Admissions officers and recruiters often search for candidates online. Scammers use public details to write convincing messages, to guess security answers or to choose targets.","## Why it lasts","Online information is easy to copy and hard to remove. A post you delete can already have been screenshotted or archived, and sites may keep copies of your data. Old accounts you have forgotten can still hold information about you.","## Check your own footprint","- Search for your name, username and email in a search engine, including images, and see what appears.\n- List your old accounts and delete the ones you no longer use.\n- Check what your social profiles show to the public.\n- Use a breach checking service to see whether your email appeared in a leak.","## Reduce and shape it","- Think before you post: would you be comfortable if a future employer or relative saw it?\n- Share less personal detail, such as your address, school schedule and birth date.\n- Use privacy settings and limit who can see your content.\n- Use separate emails or usernames for different purposes.\n- Block tracking cookies and review app permissions.\n- Ask friends not to tag you in things you would rather keep private.\n- Build a positive footprint: projects, achievements and professional profiles show who you are.","## Key takeaway","Your footprint is permanent enough to matter. Treat every post as something that could be seen later by anyone, and manage it on purpose."],"quiz":null,"cat":7},
 {"id":"photo-metadata","t":"Photo metadata: what your pictures reveal","r":"3 min read","tag":"Digital privacy and footprint","date":"October 2026","paras":["A photograph contains more than the image. Behind the scenes, most phones and cameras save extra details in the file, called metadata. The most common type is EXIF data, and it can reveal where, when and with what device the photo was taken.","## What can be inside","- Date and time the photo was taken.\n- GPS coordinates showing exactly where, if location is enabled for the camera.\n- Device make and model, and sometimes a serial number.\n- Camera settings such as exposure and orientation.\n- Editing software used.\nVideos and documents carry similar information, and Word or PDF files can contain author names and revision history.","## Why it matters","A picture of your bedroom, taken at home and shared with the location data intact, can show your home address. A photo of a new gadget can tell people where you live or when you are away. In past cases, people have been located through details embedded in images they posted. Stalkers and thieves can use this information.","## Do platforms remove it?","Many large social networks strip location metadata from images when you upload them, but not all do, and the policy can change. Sending the photo directly by email, file sharing or some messaging apps may keep the data intact. Do not assume that a platform will protect you.","## How to protect yourself","- Turn off location tagging for your camera app if you do not need it.\n- Remove metadata before sharing photos. Phones offer an option to share without location, and computers can remove properties from a file. Dedicated tools can also clear it.\n- Take screenshots of images, which usually creates a new file without the original metadata, though it may contain other details.\n- Look at the background of your photos for clues: street signs, school uniforms, house numbers, car plates and views from windows.\n- Be careful with photos of children, tickets, boarding passes and documents.","## Checking a file","On a computer, open the file properties or details tab to see what is stored. This is a good way to see what you have been sharing.","## Key takeaway","Photos can carry a map of your life. Switch off location tagging and strip metadata before posting images that you do not want traced."],"quiz":null,"cat":7},
 {"id":"privacy-settings-audit","t":"Privacy settings audits: a regular check-up for your accounts","r":"3 min read","tag":"Digital privacy and footprint","date":"October 2026","paras":["Privacy settings are the controls that decide who can see your information and what services can do with it. Defaults are often designed for convenience or for the company's benefit, not for your privacy. A regular review, an audit, takes less than an hour and removes a lot of unnecessary exposure.","## When to do it","Do an audit at least once or twice a year, after major updates to an app, when you get a new device, and after a breach affecting a service you use. Platforms change their settings and add new features, sometimes with default permissions that are open.","## What to review","- Social media: who can see your posts, friend list, photos and profile information. Limit audiences to friends, and check what tags and old posts show.\n- Search visibility: whether your profile can be found by phone number or email, and by search engines.\n- Location sharing: apps with access to your location, and map features that show it to others.\n- Connected apps and logins: third-party apps and Sign in with options that still have access to your account. Remove those you no longer use.\n- Advertising and data settings: personalised ads, data sharing and activity history.\n- Phone permissions: camera, microphone, contacts and photos for each app.\n- Browser and search: stored history, cookies, saved passwords and sync.\n- Email and cloud: shared folders, public links and forwarding rules that might send messages elsewhere.\n- Security basics: password strength, two-factor authentication, recovery email and phone, and active sessions or devices.","## How to do it","1. Make a list of the accounts you use, including old ones.\n2. Open the privacy and security section of each, and go through the options.\n3. Choose the most restrictive setting that still lets you use the service.\n4. Remove unneeded apps, devices and old posts.\n5. Delete accounts you no longer use.\n6. Note the date, and repeat later.","## Many services offer a guide","Several large platforms offer a privacy checkup tool that walks you through the key choices. These are a good starting point.","## Key takeaway","Do not rely on defaults. A regular privacy audit costs little time and reduces what strangers, advertisers and attackers can learn about you."],"quiz":null,"cat":7},
 {"id":"data-brokers","t":"Data brokers: the companies that trade in information about you","r":"3 min read","tag":"Digital privacy and footprint","date":"October 2026","paras":["Data brokers are companies that collect personal information from many sources, combine it into profiles and sell or license it to others. Most people have never heard of them, yet they may hold detailed records about you.","## Where the information comes from","- Public records, such as property and court records and voter registers, depending on the country.\n- Websites, apps and services that share or sell user data, and advertising and tracking networks.\n- Loyalty cards and purchase records.\n- Social media profiles and public posts.\n- Surveys, sweepstakes and warranty forms.\n- Other brokers, which resell and merge data.","## What a profile can contain","Name, age, address history, phone numbers, email addresses, family members, income estimates, interests, purchases, health-related interests, political leanings and location patterns. Even if each piece is minor, combined they form a detailed picture.","## Who buys it","Advertisers, marketing firms, insurers, lenders, employers, researchers and, in some places, the authorities. Some sites also publish the data openly as people-search services, which makes it easy for strangers to find your address.","## Why it is a risk","The information can help scammers to craft convincing messages and to answer security questions. It can make stalking and harassment easier, and it can feed discriminatory decisions about prices and offers. Large databases can be breached.","## What you can do","- Limit what you share, using forms, surveys and loyalty programmes selectively.\n- Use privacy settings, and decline tracking where possible.\n- Use separate emails or aliases, and consider a phone number only for important services.\n- Search for yourself on people-search sites and use their opt-out forms to request removal. This can be repetitive, because data reappears.\n- In places with privacy laws, such as the European Union, you can ask companies what they hold and request deletion. Some regions, including parts of the United States, have registries or rights to opt out of the sale of data.\n- Paid removal services exist, but check them carefully.","## Key takeaway","Your data has a market, and you are not part of the transaction. Share less, use opt-outs and your legal rights, and expect to repeat the process."],"quiz":null,"cat":7},
 {"id":"right-to-be-forgotten","t":"The right to be forgotten: asking for your data to be erased","r":"3 min read","tag":"Digital privacy and footprint","date":"October 2026","paras":["The right to be forgotten, formally called the right to erasure, is the idea that people should be able to ask companies and search engines to delete or stop publishing certain personal information about them. It is best known from European law, though similar protections exist in several other places.","## Where it comes from","In the European Union, the General Data Protection Regulation, or GDPR, gives individuals the right to ask for personal data to be erased in certain circumstances, for example when it is no longer needed for the purpose it was collected, when consent is withdrawn, or when it was processed unlawfully. A well-known 2014 ruling of the European Court of Justice also established that people can ask search engines to remove links to outdated or irrelevant information about them in some cases.","## What it does and does not mean","- It is not an absolute right to delete anything you dislike. Organisations may refuse where there are other legitimate interests, such as freedom of expression, public interest, legal obligations or reporting.\n- The content itself usually stays on the original website. A search engine may only stop showing it in results for your name.\n- It does not remove copies that others have saved or shared.\n- Rules differ between countries. Check what applies where you live.","## How to make a request","1. Find the contact for the data protection or privacy team in the company's policy.\n2. Say clearly what you want removed and why, and give links or details.\n3. Identify yourself, and keep a copy of your request and the date.\n4. For search engines, use their removal request forms.\n5. If you do not receive an answer, usually within about a month under the GDPR, or you are refused, you can complain to your national data protection authority.","## Easier steps","Delete content you control, close old accounts, ask friends to remove photos and check platform tools for removal of personal information from search results.","## Special cases","Children and young people have particular protection in many places, and requests concerning content posted when you were a minor are often easier to make.","## Key takeaway","You may have a legal right to ask for your data to be removed. It is not automatic or complete, but a clear, polite request is often worth making."],"quiz":null,"cat":7},
 {"id":"private-browsing","t":"Private browsing and anonymity: what incognito mode really does","r":"3 min read","tag":"Digital privacy and footprint","date":"October 2026","paras":["Private browsing, also called incognito mode or a private window, opens a browser session that does not save your history, cookies, site data or form entries once you close it. It is useful, but many people think that it does much more than it does.","## What it does","- Does not save the pages you visited in your browser history.\n- Deletes cookies and temporary site data at the end of the session, so you appear logged out of sites in the new session.\n- Does not keep search entries and form data on your device.\n- Useful on a shared computer, for gift shopping, for logging in to a second account, or for checking how a site looks when you are not signed in.","## What it does not do","- Websites you visit can still see your IP address and can track you during the session.\n- Your internet provider, the network owner, your school or your employer can still see which sites you visit.\n- If you log in to an account, the service knows who you are.\n- It does not protect against malware or phishing.\n- Downloaded files and bookmarks stay on the device.\n- Browser fingerprinting, which uses your device characteristics, can still recognise you.\nPrivate browsing hides your activity from other people using the same device, not from the internet.","## Private versus anonymous","Privacy means controlling who sees your information. Anonymity means that your identity cannot be linked to your activity at all. To increase privacy you can use a VPN to hide your traffic from the local network, privacy-focused browsers and search engines, tracker blockers and limited sign-ins. For stronger anonymity, tools such as the Tor Browser route traffic through several relays, though they are slower and still need careful use.","## Realistic expectations","Total anonymity is very difficult. Most people need to decide what they want to protect and from whom: other users of a device, advertisers or surveillance. Choose tools to match.","## Key takeaway","Incognito mode keeps traces off your own device and nothing more. For privacy from websites, networks or trackers you need additional tools and habits."],"quiz":null,"cat":7},
 {"id":"resume-oversharing","t":"Oversharing on résumés and forms: how much is too much?","r":"3 min read","tag":"Digital privacy and footprint","date":"October 2026","paras":["When you apply for a job, a scholarship, a course or an internship, you are asked for information. Employers and organisations often receive many applications, and documents are sometimes stored, shared or published. Giving too much can expose you to identity theft and scams.","## What does not belong on a résumé","- Full home address. A city or region is enough in most cases.\n- Date of birth, national identity or passport numbers, tax numbers and bank details.\n- Photos, marital status, religion or other protected information, unless a local convention requires some of it.\n- Social media accounts that are private or unprofessional.\n- Personal email addresses that include nicknames or birth years.\n- Very detailed information about family members.","## Online application forms","Ask whether each field is really necessary at this stage. Identity numbers and bank details are normally needed only after an offer, and through a secure process. Check that the form is on the official website and uses HTTPS, and read what the privacy policy says about storage and sharing.","## Fake opportunities","Fake job offers and scholarships collect personal details for identity fraud. Warning signs include requests for ID scans or bank details before an interview, fees to apply, and jobs that seem too good for the effort. Verify the organisation independently.","## Where your résumé goes","On job boards and public profiles, a résumé can be copied and scraped by data brokers and scammers. Use a dedicated email address and a phone number you can change, and remove your résumé from sites you no longer use.","## Good practice","- Share what is needed to show your skills, and no more.\n- Use a professional email address and a general location.\n- Provide references only with permission.\n- Do not post your résumé publicly with contact details.\n- Keep track of where you applied, and ask for deletion of data once the process is over.","## Key takeaway","A résumé should open doors, not give away your identity. Share your skills freely and your personal identifiers carefully."],"quiz":null,"cat":7}
];
